00:18:03.179 --> 00:18:05.728
So, Krav Maga it's a Hebrew, Krav means fight,

464
00:18:05.728 --> 00:18:07.757
Maga means hands, so lethal hand combat.

465
00:18:07.757 --> 00:18:09.761
So I was in that department,

466
00:18:09.761 --> 00:18:11.547
and when I was recruited into that department,

467
00:18:11.547 --> 00:18:13.457
after going through a series of tests,

468
00:18:13.457 --> 00:18:15.271
I was one of the first female instructors

469
00:18:15.271 --> 00:18:16.553
to train the commando.

470
00:18:16.553 --> 00:18:19.957
So how long between you enlisting

471
00:18:19.957 --> 00:18:22.878
and you becoming a Krav Maga instructor?

472
00:18:22.878 --> 00:18:25.020
Six months of intense, yeah.
Oh wow.

473
00:18:25.020 --> 00:18:26.522
Well, I thought maybe like two years or something.

474
00:18:26.522 --> 00:18:27.711
No, no, no.

475
00:18:27.711 --> 00:18:30.434
But it's like intense, like they don't let you go anywhere.

476
00:18:30.434 --> 00:18:33.420
Like I wasn't released to go home I think in that period,

477
00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:35.253
probably for 24 hours.

478
00:18:36.553 --> 00:18:38.332
They let me go to be a civilian

479
00:18:38.332 --> 00:18:40.358
and have a little bit of fun.

480
00:18:40.358 --> 00:18:42.225
So it starts out with some kind

481
00:18:42.225 --> 00:18:46.098
of basic training instruction and then it pivots into more

482
00:18:46.098 --> 00:18:49.302
of a special forces kind of like Seal Team situation?

483
00:18:49.302 --> 00:18:51.221
Yeah, we actually skipped the basic train,

484
00:18:51.221 --> 00:18:52.960
'cause basic training, every soldier must learn

485
00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:55.752
how to use Uzi and M-16,

486
00:18:55.752 --> 00:18:57.824
certain skills like climbing a wall, rope,

487
00:18:57.824 --> 00:18:59.709
you know, women, boys, girls, whatever,

488
00:18:59.709 --> 00:19:01.051
everyone has to learn that skill.

489
00:19:01.051 --> 00:19:02.971
We bypassed that because we went through

490
00:19:02.971 --> 00:19:06.055
a testing kind of period for a week to see what we can do.

491
00:19:06.055 --> 00:19:08.650
And the second excelled like basic training program

492
00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:12.645
and then it goes into another form of training

493
00:19:12.645 --> 00:19:14.802
that's even more intense.

494
00:19:14.802 --> 00:19:19.231
So are you the first female Krav Maga instructor

495
00:19:19.231 --> 00:19:20.165
or one of the first?

496
00:19:20.165 --> 00:19:22.793
I was the first female instructor to train the commando.

497
00:19:22.793 --> 00:19:25.558
So the commando is like the Navy Seals.

498
00:19:25.558 --> 00:19:27.876
Because what's unique about that, it's not just...

499
00:19:27.876 --> 00:19:28.859
You don't stand there with a clipboard,

500
00:19:28.859 --> 00:19:30.719
I have to do the training with them.

501
00:19:30.719 --> 00:19:33.097
And so they would send me on different...

502
00:19:33.097 --> 00:19:34.897
They'd ship me out to different locations in the country,

503
00:19:34.897 --> 00:19:36.879
I didn't even know where I was going to

504
00:19:36.879 --> 00:19:39.208
and how long I'd be for, I just knew that they'd tell me

505
00:19:39.208 --> 00:19:40.412
what I need to pack and take

506
00:19:40.412 --> 00:19:42.223
and then we go on special assignments

507
00:19:42.223 --> 00:19:45.076
and I was part of their training to train the soldiers.

508
00:19:45.076 --> 00:19:47.584
Right, and they're older I presume.

509
00:19:47.584 --> 00:19:48.417
The soldiers?

510
00:19:48.417 --> 00:19:49.506
The soldiers that you were training,

511
00:19:49.506 --> 00:19:51.042
you're 18 at this point, right?

512
00:19:51.042 --> 00:19:52.014
Yeah, I'm 18.

513
00:19:52.014 --> 00:19:53.048
Well, there are some of them,

514
00:19:53.048 --> 00:19:53.986
I mean, 'cause they're recruited too.

515
00:19:53.986 --> 00:19:56.002
So I'm like maybe not too much older than me,

516
00:19:56.002 --> 00:19:58.820
it's during the, like maybe early 20s.

517
00:19:58.820 --> 00:19:59.816
Right.
Yeah.

518
00:19:59.816 --> 00:20:02.034
And what is the qualitative differences

519
00:20:02.034 --> 00:20:05.867
between kickboxing or Taekwondo and Krav Maga?

520
00:20:07.072 --> 00:20:09.386
Well, Krav Maga it's death, right?

521
00:20:09.386 --> 00:20:12.295
You learn to use your hands in a deadly way,

522
00:20:12.295 --> 00:20:14.846
how to use a rock, a stick, a stone in a deadly way.

523
00:20:14.846 --> 00:20:16.045
Learn how to kill somebody

524
00:20:16.045 --> 00:20:19.308
so it's a little bit more intense you know.

525
00:20:19.308 --> 00:20:21.913
It's more for soldiers, right?

526
00:20:21.913 --> 00:20:24.605
You know in war in situations of life or death, right?

527
00:20:24.605 --> 00:20:28.605
So, less about art and more about self-defense

528
00:20:29.726 --> 00:20:33.419
and aggressive action when required.

529
00:20:33.419 --> 00:20:34.743
Exactly.
Under duress.

530
00:20:34.743 --> 00:20:35.849
Yes.
Yeah.

531
00:20:35.849 --> 00:20:39.697
And so how long were you in the IDF then?

532
00:20:39.697 --> 00:20:41.807
I was an instructor with the IDF

533
00:20:41.807 --> 00:20:44.336
with that department for about a year.

534
00:20:44.336 --> 00:20:45.750
And then I transitioned to work

535
00:20:45.750 --> 00:20:48.690
with the police force shortly after that.

536
00:20:48.690 --> 00:20:50.863
And during that time, I mean were you...

537
00:20:50.863 --> 00:20:53.607
So you're deployed to train other commandos

538
00:20:53.607 --> 00:20:56.778
but you weren't sort of deployed as a commando yourself?

539
00:20:56.778 --> 00:20:57.611
No.

540
00:20:57.611 --> 00:20:59.304
On secret missions and stuff like that.

541
00:20:59.304 --> 00:21:02.356
I did certain missions with the military

542
00:21:02.356 --> 00:21:04.411
but not as a commando, right?

543
00:21:04.411 --> 00:21:06.473
I couldn't, I didn't have the physical strength

544
00:21:06.473 --> 00:21:08.547
to do what they needed to do.

545
00:21:08.547 --> 00:21:09.380
You know what I mean?

546
00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:13.746
I mean, it's a very difficult like you know...

547
00:21:13.746 --> 00:21:15.808
Like commando soldiers, I'll just give you an example.

548
00:21:15.808 --> 00:21:18.938
They go through like almost two years of training

549
00:21:18.938 --> 00:21:22.372
and maybe 5% will make it, it's that intense.

550
00:21:22.372 --> 00:21:26.823
Like some of those guys will crack three hours

551
00:21:26.823 --> 00:21:28.889
before they graduate, it's that intense.

552
00:21:28.889 --> 00:21:31.024
That's our job to make them feel like nothing

553
00:21:31.024 --> 00:21:32.717
and to bring them to their lowest, right?

554
00:21:32.717 --> 00:21:35.319
So, I mean, I couldn't have done...

555
00:21:35.319 --> 00:21:36.152
You know what I mean?

556
00:21:36.152 --> 00:21:38.658
I was a trainer but to be realistic with you,

557
00:21:38.658 --> 00:21:41.157
I couldn't have been a commando soldier by any means.

558
00:21:41.157 --> 00:21:44.874
But it seems like you could have been a pretty good spy.

559
00:21:44.874 --> 00:21:45.821
I could have been, yeah.

560
00:21:45.821 --> 00:21:46.654
Maybe I was.

561
00:21:46.654 --> 00:21:48.852
Maybe you are, maybe you are right now.

562
00:21:48.852 --> 00:21:51.702
Maybe I am. (Leah laughs)
Yeah, no one knows, right?

563
00:21:51.702 --> 00:21:52.535
No.

564
00:21:52.535 --> 00:21:55.477
I mean, because you don't look typically Israeli

565
00:21:55.477 --> 00:21:58.343
and you speaking English without an accent

566
00:21:58.343 --> 00:22:00.210
and all those sorts of things, right?

567
00:22:00.210 --> 00:22:03.672
It's interesting that they didn't position you into that.

568
00:22:03.672 --> 00:22:07.491
I mean that's the James Bond dream, right?

569
00:22:07.491 --> 00:22:09.840
Well, I mean, you can wanna be a spy

570
00:22:09.840 --> 00:22:11.535
but it's not up to you, it's up to them.

571
00:22:11.535 --> 00:22:12.715
Right.
You know what I mean?

572
00:22:12.715 --> 00:22:14.913
You would seem to be a top candidate,

573
00:22:14.913 --> 00:22:16.675
they didn't recruit you for that?

574
00:22:16.675 --> 00:22:17.699
I'm not gonna say they didn't recruit me,

575
00:22:17.699 --> 00:22:18.861
I went through a series of training.

576
00:22:18.861 --> 00:22:20.109
You're gonna get cagey now.

577
00:22:20.109 --> 00:22:21.333
(Leah laughs)

578
00:22:21.333 --> 00:22:23.035
I think there's a lot more going on here.

579
00:22:23.035 --> 00:22:23.868
Maybe.

580
00:22:23.868 --> 00:22:25.471
Than you're willing to talk about.

581
00:22:25.471 --> 00:22:28.302
Yeah, I guess, I mean who knows, right?

582
00:22:28.302 --> 00:22:29.814
You can only speculate.

583
00:22:29.814 --> 00:22:34.814
Okay so, then you transitioned into this police force.

584
00:22:35.041 --> 00:22:37.278
I mean, is that...

585
00:22:37.278 --> 00:22:40.577
What does that look like in terms of like how we think

586
00:22:40.577 --> 00:22:43.162
about police in the United States, in North America.

587
00:22:43.162 --> 00:22:43.995
It's completely different.

588
00:22:43.995 --> 00:22:45.848
I mean the police and the military work together

589
00:22:45.848 --> 00:22:48.240
because the crimes of Israel are very different

590
00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:51.167
than the crimes that happen in North America.

591
00:22:51.167 --> 00:22:52.403
You know we have to deal with terrorism,

592
00:22:52.403 --> 00:22:53.807
that's our biggest threat, right?

593
00:22:53.807 --> 00:22:57.192
So, I wasn't exactly in the police force.

594
00:22:57.192 --> 00:22:58.919
I was in a unit called the Belush

595
00:22:58.919 --> 00:23:00.828
and the Belush was a spying agency,

596
00:23:00.828 --> 00:23:03.322
but I did go through like the police training,

597
00:23:03.322 --> 00:23:07.007
like the academy, like again a more intense, faster kinda

598
00:23:07.007 --> 00:23:09.134
to get me through there but I had so much skill

599
00:23:09.134 --> 00:23:10.841
from the military that I didn't have

600
00:23:10.841 --> 00:23:13.780
to do the basic training of the police academy.

601
00:23:13.780 --> 00:23:15.787
Right, so it's kind of an anti-terrorism unit.

602
00:23:15.787 --> 00:23:16.620
Correct.

603
00:23:17.527 --> 00:23:22.212
And in that context, I mean what was the day-to-day like?

604
00:23:22.212 --> 00:23:24.389
Like what are you deployed to do?

605
00:23:24.389 --> 00:23:25.946
It depends everyday.

606
00:23:25.946 --> 00:23:28.138
Was it intelligence or was it boots on the ground?

607
00:23:28.138 --> 00:23:29.109
It was intelligence,

608
00:23:29.109 --> 00:23:33.371
more internal intelligence inside the country.

609
00:23:33.371 --> 00:23:34.974
It depends if we were after a terrorist,

610
00:23:34.974 --> 00:23:38.667
if we're going into the territories, we are disguised a lot.

611
00:23:38.667 --> 00:23:40.492
You're very isolated.

612
00:23:40.492 --> 00:23:43.324
I was positioned in different parts of the country.

613
00:23:43.324 --> 00:23:47.243
You can't associate with other people and...

614
00:23:47.243 --> 00:23:48.674
You know, even if like say you and I worked together.

615
00:23:48.674 --> 00:23:50.222
Like under aliases and stuff like that?

616
00:23:50.222 --> 00:23:51.109
Yeah, exactly.

617
00:23:51.109 --> 00:23:52.955
Right, so there's some James Bond in there.

618
00:23:52.955 --> 00:23:54.807
Quite a bit of James Bond maybe, yeah.

619
00:23:54.807 --> 00:23:57.846
And how many people were in your unit?

620
00:23:57.846 --> 00:24:00.434
In the Belush there was only myself,

621
00:24:00.434 --> 00:24:02.864
I was the only woman and then there was I think,

622
00:24:02.864 --> 00:24:04.463
five other guys.

623
00:24:04.463 --> 00:24:06.536
Yeah, and can you kind of talk about

624
00:24:06.536 --> 00:24:09.792
what a typical mission might look like?

625
00:24:09.792 --> 00:24:13.379
I mean we would do a lot of surveillance

626
00:24:13.379 --> 00:24:16.830
if we had to capture someone, kidnapped someone or whatever,

627
00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:19.418
you know to bring them into Israel from the territories,

628
00:24:19.418 --> 00:24:21.295
that kind of stuff you know.

629
00:24:21.295 --> 00:24:22.469
So it's-

630
00:24:22.469 --> 00:24:23.623
That's pretty heavy.
It is.

631
00:24:23.623 --> 00:24:25.198
You're like 19 at this point?

632
00:24:25.198 --> 00:24:26.646
I'm young, yeah, I'm 20.

633
00:24:26.646 --> 00:24:28.782
Wow.
(Leah laughs)

634
00:24:28.782 --> 00:24:29.624
Yeah.

635
00:24:29.624 --> 00:24:31.677
'cause mind you to like to get into the Belush,

636
00:24:31.677 --> 00:24:34.616
like that particular unit, you need three to four years

637
00:24:34.616 --> 00:24:36.800
of police experience to even be considered

638
00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:39.537
and they recruited me after four months.

639
00:24:39.537 --> 00:24:43.772
So that was a big jump but that was kind of what I wanted.

640
00:24:43.772 --> 00:24:47.317
Like I said, you don't ask to be a spy, they come after you.

641
00:24:47.317 --> 00:24:49.933
So, they watch you, they know everything about you,

642
00:24:49.933 --> 00:24:53.259
they see how you are, how you act with other people.

643
00:24:53.259 --> 00:24:54.463
How much you can take.

644
00:24:54.463 --> 00:24:57.055
What your behavior is like when you see certain things.

645
00:24:57.055 --> 00:24:58.446
So it's a process.

646
00:24:58.446 --> 00:25:02.916
Yeah, so when you watch spy movies or Hollywood's version

647
00:25:02.916 --> 00:25:06.786
of what kind of international espionage

648
00:25:06.786 --> 00:25:10.390
and intelligence looks like, like how...

649
00:25:10.390 --> 00:25:13.619
Based on your experience, like what aspects of

650
00:25:13.619 --> 00:25:18.043
that are legitimate or look real and what is kind

651
00:25:18.043 --> 00:25:22.546
of nonsense compared to what you've seen yourself?

652
00:25:22.546 --> 00:25:24.249
Well, I mean, we don't have flying cars

653
00:25:24.249 --> 00:25:25.249
or things like that, right?

654
00:25:25.249 --> 00:25:27.107
Sure, but I mean like more grounded

655
00:25:27.107 --> 00:25:29.445
kind of (indistinct) type.

656
00:25:29.445 --> 00:25:31.535
I mean, the reality is is that you see things

657
00:25:31.535 --> 00:25:33.298
that most people don't see.

658
00:25:33.298 --> 00:25:35.098
I mean, you can be working with somebody today

659
00:25:35.098 --> 00:25:36.897
and then tomorrow they're gone like something

660
00:25:36.897 --> 00:25:39.045
so it's dangerous.

661
00:25:39.045 --> 00:25:42.423
I didn't realize what exactly how dangerous it was

662
00:25:42.423 --> 00:25:44.044
so I was like 30 years old.

663
00:25:44.044 --> 00:25:47.509
I just started feeling scared and a bit paranoid you know.

664
00:25:47.509 --> 00:25:48.608
'Cause you almost have to go in.

665
00:25:48.608 --> 00:25:51.198
'Cause if you are afraid even in inkling,

666
00:25:51.198 --> 00:25:53.472
then you can't do that type of job, right?

667
00:25:53.472 --> 00:25:54.812
But it's that dangerous.

668
00:25:54.812 --> 00:25:57.946
You know of what we're doing and the risk that we're taking.

669
00:25:57.946 --> 00:26:00.030
But you don't think that way, you just have a mission

670
00:26:00.030 --> 00:26:02.380
and you're expected to do it no matter what it takes

671
00:26:02.380 --> 00:26:03.643
if you're that patriotic

672
00:26:03.643 --> 00:26:05.004
and that's what they expect from you.

673
00:26:05.004 --> 00:26:07.423
That's why it takes a unique personality

674
00:26:07.423 --> 00:26:09.280
or person to be able to do that,

675
00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:10.896
just to put your life on the line go, you know what?

676
00:26:10.896 --> 00:26:13.795
I have a mission to do no matter what happens.

677
00:26:13.795 --> 00:26:16.186
And you did that for like 10 years then?

678
00:26:16.186 --> 00:26:17.019
Almost 10 years.

679
00:26:17.019 --> 00:26:17.970
Yeah.
Correct.

680
00:26:17.970 --> 00:26:18.803
Yeah.

681
00:26:20.095 --> 00:26:23.027
That's gotta be challenging, right?

682
00:26:23.027 --> 00:26:23.967
Especially in terms

683
00:26:23.967 --> 00:26:26.745
of like maintaining relationships with people.

684
00:26:26.745 --> 00:26:27.934
There is no relationship with people.

685
00:26:27.934 --> 00:26:29.901
Yeah, you can't be tied to anybody, right?

686
00:26:29.901 --> 00:26:33.602
I'd also be like I worked with a lot of...

687
00:26:33.602 --> 00:26:35.626
My partners had wives and children.

688
00:26:35.626 --> 00:26:38.474
I would be terrified if I had a child because of-

689
00:26:38.474 --> 00:26:39.312
Its leverage.

690
00:26:39.312 --> 00:26:41.483
Yeah, the nature of the work, you know it's worse

691
00:26:41.483 --> 00:26:44.350
when something happens to your loved then it happens to you.

692
00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:46.170
So, being single and independent

693
00:26:46.170 --> 00:26:47.981
and my family being way way out there,

694
00:26:47.981 --> 00:26:49.846
and not having anyone write me letters

695
00:26:49.846 --> 00:26:51.845
or even when I was talking with my parents,

696
00:26:51.845 --> 00:26:54.308
I wanted to keep it as rare as possible.

697
00:26:54.308 --> 00:26:57.432
And even to leave the country is as little as possible.

698
00:26:57.432 --> 00:26:59.668
For me, just from what the experience that I saw,

699
00:26:59.668 --> 00:27:01.001
it kept me....

700
00:27:01.001 --> 00:27:02.378
I just felt safer that way.

701
00:27:02.378 --> 00:27:03.211
Yeah.

702
00:27:03.211 --> 00:27:05.650
Your parents knew what you were doing on some level though.

703
00:27:05.650 --> 00:27:08.964
To a degree, I think my mom knew more than my father.

704
00:27:08.964 --> 00:27:10.800
Yeah, what was their perspective?

705
00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:12.566
Not great, and well my mom for sure,

706
00:27:12.566 --> 00:27:13.713
she was all for it, right?

707
00:27:13.713 --> 00:27:17.802
But my father was afraid because he knew people that...

708
00:27:17.802 --> 00:27:19.888
I mean when I came back to Canada very briefly,

709
00:27:19.888 --> 00:27:22.749
we had a private conversation and he just said that,

710
00:27:22.749 --> 00:27:25.279
he knew people that escaped that kind of work.

711
00:27:25.279 --> 00:27:28.090
That they left because it just and I guess tried

712
00:27:28.090 --> 00:27:30.213
to scare me out of it but it didn't matter

713
00:27:30.213 --> 00:27:32.052
what anyone said I knew what I was gonna do

714
00:27:32.052 --> 00:27:33.449
and I was gonna do it no matter what.

715
00:27:33.449 --> 00:27:34.282
Yeah.

716
00:27:36.222 --> 00:27:39.055
(dramatic music)

717
00:28:10.636 --> 00:28:12.286
And did you feel like you were fulfilling

718
00:28:12.286 --> 00:28:14.119
that James Bond dream.

719
00:28:16.571 --> 00:28:19.908 line:15%
To a degree, yes I messed up once.

720
00:28:19.908 --> 00:28:23.052 line:15%
I think I spoke when I shouldn't have.

721
00:28:23.052 --> 00:28:27.488
And so, I mean I was in a process of getting recruited.

722
00:28:27.488 --> 00:28:30.928
I'm not saying to what organization, you know what I mean

723
00:28:30.928 --> 00:28:33.337
but it was probably the most elite.

724
00:28:33.337 --> 00:28:34.414
The one that I'm thinking of.

725
00:28:34.414 --> 00:28:36.111
Yeah, the one that you're thinking of right now

726
00:28:36.111 --> 00:28:38.063
and it was a series of six months,

727
00:28:38.063 --> 00:28:42.286
I was going through a series of mental and physical tests

728
00:28:42.286 --> 00:28:43.841
and I didn't know...

729
00:28:43.841 --> 00:28:44.813
I didn't ask any questions,

730
00:28:44.813 --> 00:28:47.646
I just get these notices or calls,

731
00:28:49.265 --> 00:28:52.054
you're gonna be at this address at this time

732
00:28:52.054 --> 00:28:54.661
in Tel Aviv or in Haifa, or in Jerusalem or whatever,

733
00:28:54.661 --> 00:28:56.379
you just show up and you don't say anything,

734
00:28:56.379 --> 00:28:58.240
you do whatever you're supposed to do and then you leave

735
00:28:58.240 --> 00:28:59.579
and you don't know if you're gonna get a call

736
00:28:59.579 --> 00:29:00.642
or not gonna get a call.

737
00:29:00.642 --> 00:29:02.691
And you don't know why you're being dispatched?

738
00:29:02.691 --> 00:29:05.014
You kinda know why but you just don't know

739
00:29:05.014 --> 00:29:07.354
what's happening or what's gonna come next.

740
00:29:07.354 --> 00:29:09.313
I mean at one point I had waited a month

741
00:29:09.313 --> 00:29:11.676
and then I got another notice, right?

742
00:29:11.676 --> 00:29:13.577
So it's that kind of process

743
00:29:13.577 --> 00:29:18.506
and then kind of at the end of that, I called my uncle.

744
00:29:18.506 --> 00:29:21.015
Because I wanted to ask him what the heck is going on,

745
00:29:21.015 --> 00:29:22.832
and that was a mistake.

746
00:29:22.832 --> 00:29:25.930
Your uncle being part of intelligence.

747
00:29:25.930 --> 00:29:26.942
Perhaps.

748
00:29:26.942 --> 00:29:28.327
Okay, right.

749
00:29:28.327 --> 00:29:29.580
I'm trying to just understand this.

750
00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:32.376
So, basically you spoke out of school, you weren't supposed

751
00:29:32.376 --> 00:29:36.102
to say anything and just by having a conversation.

752
00:29:36.102 --> 00:29:37.887
Oh yeah, I mean as soon as I...

753
00:29:37.887 --> 00:29:39.825
We sat down actually on a table very similar to this,

754
00:29:39.825 --> 00:29:40.894
it was a picnic table outside

755
00:29:40.894 --> 00:29:44.129
in a little village called Afula.

756
00:29:44.129 --> 00:29:47.061
And even before I opened my mouth he just shook his head.

757
00:29:47.061 --> 00:29:49.680
And then I said, okay, I just messed up.

758
00:29:49.680 --> 00:29:51.040
And it was very difficult

759
00:29:51.040 --> 00:29:51.884
'cause I remember this was my dream.

760
00:29:51.884 --> 00:29:54.135
Because you should never broach any of this.

761
00:29:54.135 --> 00:29:56.563
You don't say anything to anyone.

762
00:29:56.563 --> 00:30:00.872
So, you're immediately flagged as a security risk.

763
00:30:00.872 --> 00:30:04.334
I think so to a degree like can't keep your trap sharp,

764
00:30:04.334 --> 00:30:05.694
you know what I mean but...

765
00:30:05.694 --> 00:30:08.669
And so, but you're young and I was so excited and eager

766
00:30:08.669 --> 00:30:10.191
and just want to know what was going on

767
00:30:10.191 --> 00:30:12.181
and if I was gonna be recruited and I was willing

768
00:30:12.181 --> 00:30:16.568
to do anything so, it was a blow, it was a hard blow for me.

769
00:30:16.568 --> 00:30:18.843
It had to be kind of romantic and exciting

770
00:30:18.843 --> 00:30:20.842
but also terrifying.

771
00:30:20.842 --> 00:30:22.659
And I'm just curious about

772
00:30:22.659 --> 00:30:26.326
what it would feel like to be in a situation

773
00:30:27.357 --> 00:30:29.767
where you know you're being watched all the time.

774
00:30:29.767 --> 00:30:31.758
Like that's kind of a feel oppressive,

775
00:30:31.758 --> 00:30:33.321
like knowing everything that you say,

776
00:30:33.321 --> 00:30:37.204
everything that you're doing is being observed and analyzed.

777
00:30:37.204 --> 00:30:39.755
Well, I mean I think it's for the security.

778
00:30:39.755 --> 00:30:42.724
If you choose to do that kind of work they have to know,

779
00:30:42.724 --> 00:30:44.252
perhaps you're a double agent who knows,

780
00:30:44.252 --> 00:30:45.562
they don't know who you are.

781
00:30:45.562 --> 00:30:48.602
Like why would a Canadian girl live in a great country,

782
00:30:48.602 --> 00:30:51.158
come to Israel and wanna do a work, like what the hell?

783
00:30:51.158 --> 00:30:51.991
You know what I mean?

784
00:30:51.991 --> 00:30:52.883
You've been planted there.

785
00:30:52.883 --> 00:30:54.334
Exactly, like what?

786
00:30:54.334 --> 00:30:55.594
That doesn't make any sense

787
00:30:55.594 --> 00:30:58.919
and that's why I was interviewed maybe 50 times.

788
00:30:58.919 --> 00:31:00.370
Like you know what's wrong with you?

789
00:31:00.370 --> 00:31:01.203
You know what I mean?

790
00:31:01.203 --> 00:31:02.036
So, there're suspects.

791
00:31:02.036 --> 00:31:02.869
Well, I mean-

792
00:31:02.869 --> 00:31:04.433
And also you're like the only woman, right?

793
00:31:04.433 --> 00:31:05.796
Yeah, I mean exactly.

794
00:31:05.796 --> 00:31:06.982
So what was that like?

795
00:31:06.982 --> 00:31:09.467
That was tough, for sure I had my challenges,

796
00:31:09.467 --> 00:31:12.694
I had to be even better than everyone else

797
00:31:12.694 --> 00:31:15.143
because I was a woman 'cause if I had a bad day,

798
00:31:15.143 --> 00:31:16.755
it wasn't because I had a bad day it's

799
00:31:16.755 --> 00:31:18.220
because I was a woman, right?

800
00:31:18.220 --> 00:31:21.057
So, you think about that pressure of always being

801
00:31:21.057 --> 00:31:23.684
on top of your game having had eyes in the back of your head

802
00:31:23.684 --> 00:31:26.291
and yeah, it's a lot of pressure.

803
00:31:26.291 --> 00:31:29.529
What was your kind of specific skill set,

804
00:31:29.529 --> 00:31:33.356
like what was your strength in that capacity?

805
00:31:33.356 --> 00:31:36.457
I'm kind of like a chameleon, I can be in places not...

806
00:31:36.457 --> 00:31:39.196
Being in a place to not be known or seen or whatever

807
00:31:39.196 --> 00:31:42.232
and I could, good at getting information.

808
00:31:42.232 --> 00:31:45.523
It's funny 'cause I'm not a super social person,

809
00:31:45.523 --> 00:31:48.022
but if I need to socialize and talk to someone

810
00:31:48.022 --> 00:31:49.960
in an acting scenario, I can do it,

811
00:31:49.960 --> 00:31:50.898
I'm really good at it.

812
00:31:50.898 --> 00:31:55.797
So that kind of stuff, I mean, a lot of times to going into,

813
00:31:55.797 --> 00:31:57.921
like a good example is going into the territories

814
00:31:57.921 --> 00:31:59.587
when you were going into a house

815
00:31:59.587 --> 00:32:01.150
and then there's a Muslim woman there,

816
00:32:01.150 --> 00:32:02.880
when they see a woman come in,

817
00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:04.803
it kind of diffuses the situation,

818
00:32:04.803 --> 00:32:07.516
it makes them a little bit more relaxed.

819
00:32:07.516 --> 00:32:09.852
So yeah, it was just, it depends.

820
00:32:09.852 --> 00:32:13.988
Yeah. I suspect you have great situational awareness.

821
00:32:13.988 --> 00:32:16.374
Like you're trained to walk into a room

822
00:32:16.374 --> 00:32:19.134
and notice everything and kind of be aware of

823
00:32:19.134 --> 00:32:20.489
where everyone's standing

824
00:32:20.489 --> 00:32:23.076
and assess everybody's motivations.

825
00:32:23.076 --> 00:32:25.061
That's a big part of the training though.

826
00:32:25.061 --> 00:32:27.263
Like we know, I mean when I was being trained

827
00:32:27.263 --> 00:32:29.447
into the Belush, we'd go down like in Hadera

828
00:32:29.447 --> 00:32:31.776
or another village and you know one

829
00:32:31.776 --> 00:32:33.525
of the agents would point to different people

830
00:32:33.525 --> 00:32:36.184
and count Muslim, Israeli, Russian,

831
00:32:36.184 --> 00:32:37.599
what nationality, you know what I mean?

832
00:32:37.599 --> 00:32:39.486
Or like me and you having a conversation,

833
00:32:39.486 --> 00:32:42.158
listening to the people behind us what they're saying.

834
00:32:42.158 --> 00:32:43.472
So kind of being aware.

835
00:32:43.472 --> 00:32:46.658
And a lot of times when we were in the police car,

836
00:32:46.658 --> 00:32:48.674
they'd purposely put the radio on really loud

837
00:32:48.674 --> 00:32:50.499
'cause we always have to hear communication

838
00:32:50.499 --> 00:32:51.909
and I'd put it down to no, no, no,

839
00:32:51.909 --> 00:32:54.884
you have to learn to focus your energies

840
00:32:54.884 --> 00:32:56.984
in different areas even being distracted, right?

841
00:32:56.984 --> 00:32:58.559
Which is really hard for me.

842
00:32:58.559 --> 00:33:00.141
Like if I blair on the music right now

843
00:33:00.141 --> 00:33:01.314
and you and I have a conversation,

844
00:33:01.314 --> 00:33:04.189
it's gonna be tough to focus, right?

845
00:33:04.189 --> 00:33:07.049
And what was the physical training like?

846
00:33:07.049 --> 00:33:09.622
The physical, that actually came easy to me.

847
00:33:09.622 --> 00:33:10.455
'Cause I was...

848
00:33:10.455 --> 00:33:11.310
I mean I-

849
00:33:11.310 --> 00:33:14.798
I'm curious about how that plays into the cycling career

850
00:33:14.798 --> 00:33:16.167
which we're gonna get into in a little bit.

851
00:33:16.167 --> 00:33:18.323
Well remember, we didn't talk about this

852
00:33:18.323 --> 00:33:21.916
but in the military I was actually introduced to the bike

853
00:33:21.916 --> 00:33:23.977
by a Lieutenant from the commando.

854
00:33:23.977 --> 00:33:26.727
He was the national champion in the sport of triathlon.

855
00:33:26.727 --> 00:33:31.163
And he knew what I was doing, I mean to a degree.

856
00:33:31.163 --> 00:33:32.287
But he always saw me training,

857
00:33:32.287 --> 00:33:34.725
so when I wasn't training troops, I was training myself

858
00:33:34.725 --> 00:33:38.023
through the obstacles and what we needed to be good at,

859
00:33:38.023 --> 00:33:39.827
in shooting range and what not.

860
00:33:39.827 --> 00:33:41.257
So he asked me to...

861
00:33:41.257 --> 00:33:43.574
And I used to commute to the base on my bike.

862
00:33:43.574 --> 00:33:45.445
So he says, you know let's go for a bike ride.

863
00:33:45.445 --> 00:33:48.310
And I really didn't want to but I felt bad to say no

864
00:33:48.310 --> 00:33:50.521
so we went on a bike ride and I was hooked,

865
00:33:50.521 --> 00:33:52.398
I just like fell in love with the bike.

866
00:33:52.398 --> 00:33:53.671
And then he started actually coaching me

867
00:33:53.671 --> 00:33:56.399
and he got me into the sport of duathlons, right?

868
00:33:56.399 --> 00:33:59.472
And I remember my first duathlon, I was a commando soldier

869
00:33:59.472 --> 00:34:01.334
and I remember, you know how I warmed up for it?

870
00:34:01.334 --> 00:34:03.246
You know how most people would ride or run?

871
00:34:03.246 --> 00:34:07.301
My commander came with focus pads and I was punching.

872
00:34:07.301 --> 00:34:10.722
You like shadow boxing to get ready for an endurance race.

873
00:34:10.722 --> 00:34:14.545
So I did the duathlon, as soon as I crossed whatever line,

874
00:34:14.545 --> 00:34:16.517
I didn't have chance, he basically threw my bike

875
00:34:16.517 --> 00:34:18.696
in the back of the Jeep and we went back to the base.

876
00:34:18.696 --> 00:34:20.869
That's how I was able to do races

877
00:34:20.869 --> 00:34:23.796
with my security team coming with me.

878
00:34:23.796 --> 00:34:25.345
So how did that first race go?

879
00:34:25.345 --> 00:34:26.646
It was good, I actually won it.

880
00:34:26.646 --> 00:34:27.896
(Leah laughs)

881
00:34:27.896 --> 00:34:29.704
You are unbelievable.

882
00:34:29.704 --> 00:34:31.846
I mean okay, that gets Israel.

883
00:34:31.846 --> 00:34:34.114
You know I was a big fish in a small pond.

884
00:34:34.114 --> 00:34:37.142
You probably had a junky bike, right?

885
00:34:37.142 --> 00:34:38.731
I had a 20 pound tank, right.

886
00:34:38.731 --> 00:34:40.915
The big hairdryer helmet, you know back then

887
00:34:40.915 --> 00:34:42.507
and I didn't know what clipless shoes was,

888
00:34:42.507 --> 00:34:44.424
I had running shoes on.

889
00:34:45.783 --> 00:34:48.872
So, did some lights go on in that moment,

890
00:34:48.872 --> 00:34:51.829
like this could be a cool thing for me to pursue.

891
00:34:51.829 --> 00:34:53.142
Oh totally, I wanted...

892
00:34:53.142 --> 00:34:57.064
Any opportunity I had for training, we went on a bike ride

893
00:34:57.064 --> 00:35:00.705
and then I started to upgrade and learn about the bike.

894
00:35:00.705 --> 00:35:02.933
And I actually won my Duathlon,

895
00:35:02.933 --> 00:35:05.055
it wasn't because of the running I was a good runner

896
00:35:05.055 --> 00:35:07.718
but not a great runner, it was because of the riding.

897
00:35:07.718 --> 00:35:09.700
And it just something that I fell in love.

898
00:35:09.700 --> 00:35:10.533
Yeah.

899
00:35:10.533 --> 00:35:13.795
And did you become like national champion in duathlon?

900
00:35:13.795 --> 00:35:14.628
Yes, I was.

901
00:35:14.628 --> 00:35:17.666
(both laughs)

902
00:35:17.666 --> 00:35:19.342
How long between that first race

903
00:35:19.342 --> 00:35:22.406
and becoming Israeli national champion in duathlon?

904
00:35:22.406 --> 00:35:23.980
Well, I remember I couldn't go to all the races

905
00:35:23.980 --> 00:35:25.244
because of whatever I was doing,

906
00:35:25.244 --> 00:35:26.768
I had to get special permission by-

907
00:35:26.768 --> 00:35:29.078
Can't do it, I'm undercover or it's unknown.

908
00:35:29.078 --> 00:35:30.492
Well, they didn't say that

909
00:35:30.492 --> 00:35:33.278
but when there was like big races,

910
00:35:33.278 --> 00:35:35.394
they would give me releases so on the national championship

911
00:35:35.394 --> 00:35:37.730
that was roughly a year later,

912
00:35:37.730 --> 00:35:39.308
that I was able to win that race.

913
00:35:39.308 --> 00:35:40.141
Within a year?

914
00:35:40.141 --> 00:35:41.754
Yeah, within a year.
Wow.

915
00:35:41.754 --> 00:35:46.754
But then at some point you retire from the police force.

916
00:35:46.966 --> 00:35:47.799
Correct.

917
00:35:47.799 --> 00:35:50.457
And the sense that I got, I couldn't find a lot of detail

918
00:35:50.457 --> 00:35:52.470
on this but the sense that I got was

919
00:35:52.470 --> 00:35:54.819
that things were getting crazy, dangerous

920
00:35:54.819 --> 00:35:56.908
and somebody in your unit was killed

921
00:35:56.908 --> 00:36:01.557
and there was quite a looming threat concerning your life.

922
00:36:01.557 --> 00:36:02.390
Correct, yeah.

923
00:36:02.390 --> 00:36:05.842
I mean, at this point there was I think a couple

924
00:36:05.842 --> 00:36:07.355
of kidnapping attempts on me.

925
00:36:07.355 --> 00:36:09.095
And remember Israel is a very small country

926
00:36:09.095 --> 00:36:10.865
and is hard to hide me.

927
00:36:10.865 --> 00:36:13.741
'Cause you being the only woman doing what I was doing.

928
00:36:13.741 --> 00:36:17.304
And there was a lot of tension in the country at the time.

929
00:36:17.304 --> 00:36:19.817
And just one incident, I was working the night shift

930
00:36:19.817 --> 00:36:22.656
with my partner and we saw a police cruiser parked

931
00:36:22.656 --> 00:36:25.170
on the side and two officers were sleeping with their,

932
00:36:25.170 --> 00:36:27.579
kinda hugging their M-16s, which you can't do,

933
00:36:27.579 --> 00:36:30.191
it's very dangerous in the area that we were in.

934
00:36:30.191 --> 00:36:31.555
And we went to the car and we said,

935
00:36:31.555 --> 00:36:33.243
you know what are you doing but it's a different department,

936
00:36:33.243 --> 00:36:36.218
so we basically left and my partner was just yelling

937
00:36:36.218 --> 00:36:37.877
at them, like you guys are stupid.

938
00:36:37.877 --> 00:36:40.291
45 minutes later, they were assassinated.

939
00:36:40.291 --> 00:36:42.668
And all hell break loose in the country.

940
00:36:42.668 --> 00:36:46.453
And the next day too, my department gets a picture

941
00:36:46.453 --> 00:36:50.276
of me saying that, oh, like threats of...

942
00:36:50.276 --> 00:36:51.552
That I was a target.

943
00:36:51.552 --> 00:36:53.703
So they had to hide me at that point, right?

944
00:36:53.703 --> 00:36:55.930
And just knowing that I couldn't go anywhere

945
00:36:55.930 --> 00:36:59.851
without being fully armed or going into my car

946
00:36:59.851 --> 00:37:01.689
and checking underneath, over, whatever.

947
00:37:01.689 --> 00:37:02.868
Even starting my car was...

948
00:37:02.868 --> 00:37:04.780
My heart would start to beat whatever,

949
00:37:04.780 --> 00:37:06.303
they rigged or do you know.

950
00:37:06.303 --> 00:37:08.580
So, I started to become paranoid and afraid

951
00:37:08.580 --> 00:37:11.950
and so I said, I need to transition.

952
00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:14.693
Honestly, the only thing that really made me feel alive

953
00:37:14.693 --> 00:37:15.952
and the only thing I wanted to do at

954
00:37:15.952 --> 00:37:18.440
that time was be a pro racer.

955
00:37:18.440 --> 00:37:20.673
'Cause I felt like I was good enough to do it,

956
00:37:20.673 --> 00:37:23.953
I was winning races since I was, winning races against men.

957
00:37:23.953 --> 00:37:26.713
Remember I'm in a very small country not really knowing

958
00:37:26.713 --> 00:37:28.959
what real competition is.

959
00:37:28.959 --> 00:37:31.110
But time's up in terms of your safety

960
00:37:31.110 --> 00:37:32.872
and you've gotta pull the escape hatch.

961
00:37:32.872 --> 00:37:33.847
Well, yeah, they couldn't hide,

962
00:37:33.847 --> 00:37:35.487
where are they gonna hide me?

963
00:37:35.487 --> 00:37:37.925
I mean I was transferred now into the headquarters

964
00:37:37.925 --> 00:37:41.275
to train high officials like officers and whatnot.

965
00:37:41.275 --> 00:37:44.136
But I felt like I was a trainer and that's not what I was...

966
00:37:44.136 --> 00:37:45.796
What I had signed up for.

967
00:37:45.796 --> 00:37:47.663
I wanted to work the field but...

968
00:37:47.663 --> 00:37:50.146
So I basically, to make a long story short,

969
00:37:50.146 --> 00:37:51.900
I made a deal with them to work on assignment

970
00:37:51.900 --> 00:37:54.472
if needed outside of the country which allowed me

971
00:37:54.472 --> 00:37:56.321
to come back to North America

972
00:37:56.321 --> 00:37:58.522
and then I could train full-time as a cyclist.

973
00:37:58.522 --> 00:38:00.619
So, what year was that?

974
00:38:00.619 --> 00:38:02.022
Oh my goodness!

975
00:38:02.022 --> 00:38:03.900
It would be in the '80s, late '80s.

976
00:38:03.900 --> 00:38:05.383
Late '80s, right.

977
00:38:05.383 --> 00:38:07.084
And you're like 30 at the time?

978
00:38:07.084 --> 00:38:07.923
I'm 30, yeah.

979
00:38:07.923 --> 00:38:09.347
So you moved back to Canada.

980
00:38:09.347 --> 00:38:10.704
Correct, yeah.

981
00:38:10.704 --> 00:38:11.894
And you get on the bike.

982
00:38:11.894 --> 00:38:14.167
I get on the bike.
Right.

983
00:38:14.167 --> 00:38:16.443
Thinking that I got this.

984
00:38:16.443 --> 00:38:17.944
Was it duathlon at the time

985
00:38:17.944 --> 00:38:19.675
or just I'm gonna be a cyclist?

986
00:38:19.675 --> 00:38:20.965
I wanted to be a pro, I want to be a pro racer.

987
00:38:20.965 --> 00:38:22.064
You wanted to be a pro cyclist?

988
00:38:22.064 --> 00:38:23.383
Yeah, pro cyclist.

989
00:38:23.383 --> 00:38:25.883
So how do you even begin to pursue that?

990
00:38:25.883 --> 00:38:27.719
Well for one, it was a hard transition.

991
00:38:27.719 --> 00:38:30.172
You know you think about the life that I'm just leaving

992
00:38:30.172 --> 00:38:33.344
to become a normal citizen you know?

993
00:38:33.344 --> 00:38:35.011
So it took a bit of time for that.

994
00:38:35.011 --> 00:38:38.665
And you hadn't spent decades riding your bike around?

995
00:38:38.665 --> 00:38:40.471
Coming up in the traditional sense.

996
00:38:40.471 --> 00:38:42.195
No.
You're 30 at this point

997
00:38:42.195 --> 00:38:43.619
and you're like, I'm gonna be a pro cyclist?

998
00:38:43.619 --> 00:38:45.155
And people thought I was insane.

999
00:38:45.155 --> 00:38:45.988
Like what the hell?

1000
00:38:45.988 --> 00:38:48.608
Like even like the president

1001
00:38:48.608 --> 00:38:50.394
of the National Acadian Cycling Team

1002
00:38:50.394 --> 00:38:52.207
or the Federation I should say, right?

1003
00:38:52.207 --> 00:38:54.056
He says, you're not zero, you've missed the boat,

1004
00:38:54.056 --> 00:38:56.178
you're too old, you can't sprint very well,

1005
00:38:56.178 --> 00:38:57.208
your mentality is...

1006
00:38:57.208 --> 00:38:59.233
I mean he didn't know me right you know.

1007
00:38:59.233 --> 00:39:00.796
And they basically shunned me off

1008
00:39:00.796 --> 00:39:04.210
so I had to prove to them that even though I was 30

1009
00:39:04.210 --> 00:39:07.410
which is basically the end of a lot of careers in cycling,

1010
00:39:07.410 --> 00:39:10.069
and here I am wanting to start it that I have this

1011
00:39:10.069 --> 00:39:13.559
and I can do this sport on a national, international level.

1012
00:39:13.559 --> 00:39:16.146
So how did you plead your case?

1013
00:39:16.146 --> 00:39:18.744
I had to go out there and show them who I was

1014
00:39:18.744 --> 00:39:21.906
and I'm telling you it was embarrassing because...

1015
00:39:21.906 --> 00:39:23.822
I mean, in the first part of pro racing

1016
00:39:23.822 --> 00:39:26.993
I excelled very fast to, you know it's got categories.

1017
00:39:26.993 --> 00:39:29.844
But when you hit the pro level of whatever racing,

1018
00:39:29.844 --> 00:39:32.345
I mean I would go into these races and I was relying

1019
00:39:32.345 --> 00:39:35.432
on my fitness but cycling is very much like a chess game.

1020
00:39:35.432 --> 00:39:37.482
You have to think three moves before you make your own move.

1021
00:39:37.482 --> 00:39:38.643
It's not a matter of who's the strongest,

1022
00:39:38.643 --> 00:39:41.389
it's who's the smartest.

1023
00:39:41.389 --> 00:39:43.806
And I go into these races and I come in so last

1024
00:39:43.806 --> 00:39:45.366
that I wouldn't even know where the finish was.

1025
00:39:45.366 --> 00:39:47.067
I'd see my car in an empty parking lot,

1026
00:39:47.067 --> 00:39:48.156
okay yeah, this must be it.

1027
00:39:48.156 --> 00:39:51.150
Yeah, so you go from being kind of a big fish

1028
00:39:51.150 --> 00:39:53.324
in a small pond to immediately realizing

1029
00:39:53.324 --> 00:39:56.290
that the pond is quite large.

1030
00:39:56.290 --> 00:39:59.524
Yeah, I'm a shrimp in an ocean now.

1031
00:39:59.524 --> 00:40:02.134
So how do you bridge that gap?

1032
00:40:02.134 --> 00:40:05.192
Well, at this point like I'm now 38 years old, right?

1033
00:40:05.192 --> 00:40:08.131
And I'm kinda making myself sound worse than what I was,

1034
00:40:08.131 --> 00:40:11.118
but I was racing for a pro team, a U.S. pro team.

1035
00:40:11.118 --> 00:40:13.279
I was at a big race in Redlands, California

1036
00:40:13.279 --> 00:40:15.549
and I heard a team director talking about a rider,

1037
00:40:15.549 --> 00:40:17.300
remember I'm 38 now, right?

1038
00:40:17.300 --> 00:40:19.510
But what happened between 30 and 38.

1039
00:40:19.510 --> 00:40:20.343
I'm struggling.

1040
00:40:20.343 --> 00:40:22.073
I'm wanting to-
But you're staying in it?

1041
00:40:22.073 --> 00:40:23.334
I'm staying in it because I-

1042
00:40:23.334 --> 00:40:24.908
So for eight years you're trying

1043
00:40:24.908 --> 00:40:27.060
to become this pro cyclist-

1044
00:40:27.060 --> 00:40:28.522
You bet.
Unsuccessfully.

1045
00:40:28.522 --> 00:40:30.336
Because I never quit anything in my life

1046
00:40:30.336 --> 00:40:31.471
and I'm determined to get there.

1047
00:40:31.471 --> 00:40:34.578
I mean also it contrast to like,

1048
00:40:34.578 --> 00:40:36.508
hey, listen, every sport I sign up for

1049
00:40:36.508 --> 00:40:38.959
I'm the world champion within months.

1050
00:40:38.959 --> 00:40:40.809
So this is a humbling-

1051
00:40:40.809 --> 00:40:42.295
Oh, totally.
Change of pace.

1052
00:40:42.295 --> 00:40:44.423
It was completely something I'd never experienced before

1053
00:40:44.423 --> 00:40:46.418
but a good lesson though.

1054
00:40:46.418 --> 00:40:48.598
But in honesty, like I was saying, I was in a race

1055
00:40:48.598 --> 00:40:51.061
and a team director was talking about a rider.

1056
00:40:51.061 --> 00:40:53.249
And he basically said, when the road goes up,

1057
00:40:53.249 --> 00:40:55.849
she drops down, which means he's referring to climbing.

1058
00:40:55.849 --> 00:40:58.534
And those five words that I'd never forget till the day

1059
00:40:58.534 --> 00:41:01.775
I die is Goldstein is my last name, can't climb more shit.

1060
00:41:01.775 --> 00:41:03.063
And everyone starts laughing,

1061
00:41:03.063 --> 00:41:05.324
not just him but other directors and coaches

1062
00:41:05.324 --> 00:41:07.050
and other riders and whatnot.

1063
00:41:07.050 --> 00:41:09.800
And it was at that moment that I said, you know what,

1064
00:41:09.800 --> 00:41:12.336
I'm gonna show you guys exactly what I can do.

1065
00:41:12.336 --> 00:41:16.472
And I basically in that one year, I hired a climbing coach.

1066
00:41:16.472 --> 00:41:18.710
I moved to like from Vancouver to Vernon

1067
00:41:18.710 --> 00:41:21.025
which is more hilly, bike friendly location,

1068
00:41:21.025 --> 00:41:22.323
I dropped about 10 pounds.

1069
00:41:22.323 --> 00:41:25.533
And I came back the following season at 38 years old,

1070
00:41:25.533 --> 00:41:28.831
not only winning hilly races but setting new records.

1071
00:41:28.831 --> 00:41:31.219
So it was that transition that fast.

1072
00:41:31.219 --> 00:41:33.608
At 38 competing against women who are

1073
00:41:33.608 --> 00:41:35.584
probably 10 years your junior.

1074
00:41:35.584 --> 00:41:37.256
Oh way, oh, yeah.
Yeah.

1075
00:41:37.256 --> 00:41:39.690
You need somebody to just follow you around and tell you,

1076
00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:40.829
you can't do stuff.

1077
00:41:40.829 --> 00:41:42.179
I know, that's what I'm saying.

1078
00:41:42.179 --> 00:41:43.692
You should pay somebody to do that.

1079
00:41:43.692 --> 00:41:45.659
I love when people say I can't do things.

1080
00:41:45.659 --> 00:41:47.502
(Leah laughs)

1081
00:41:47.502 --> 00:41:51.221
But wow, I didn't realize there was an eight year period

1082
00:41:51.221 --> 00:41:55.559
of just kind of utter humiliation and staying in it

1083
00:41:55.559 --> 00:41:56.908
and struggling but persisting.

1084
00:41:56.908 --> 00:41:59.934
It was a tough go, it was really, really tough.

1085
00:41:59.934 --> 00:42:02.668
And there's nothing really romantic

1086
00:42:02.668 --> 00:42:05.492
about pro cycling at that level.

1087
00:42:05.492 --> 00:42:08.222
Like it's a grind and there's no money.

1088
00:42:08.222 --> 00:42:11.205
But you were on a team like where you getting a stipend?

1089
00:42:11.205 --> 00:42:12.519
Were you able to support yourself

1090
00:42:12.519 --> 00:42:14.490
or did you have a job doing something else?

1091
00:42:14.490 --> 00:42:17.319
Well, I was smart when I was younger I saved my money

1092
00:42:17.319 --> 00:42:19.093
and I got into real estate very young.

1093
00:42:19.093 --> 00:42:21.590
When I came back, when I left Israel came back to Canada,

1094
00:42:21.590 --> 00:42:23.533
it was more affordable back then, right?

1095
00:42:23.533 --> 00:42:27.849
So I started to invest 'cause I was a saver.

1096
00:42:27.849 --> 00:42:31.659
And I was a trainer, I worked at gyms and stuff but-

1097
00:42:31.659 --> 00:42:33.821
So you're training like combat sports

1098
00:42:33.821 --> 00:42:35.973
but you're competing on the bike.

1099
00:42:35.973 --> 00:42:36.858
Right, exactly.

1100
00:42:36.858 --> 00:42:37.858
Yeah, wow.

1101
00:42:39.319 --> 00:42:40.450
That's interesting too just

1102
00:42:40.450 --> 00:42:42.971
from a physiological perspective because cycling

1103
00:42:42.971 --> 00:42:46.818
is all about just dropping as much weight in the upper body

1104
00:42:46.818 --> 00:42:49.221
as possible and being skinny as possible.

1105
00:42:49.221 --> 00:42:51.258
Exactly. But like I said though,

1106
00:42:51.258 --> 00:42:53.131
I mean, it's very much a team sport too, pro racing,

1107
00:42:53.131 --> 00:42:55.112
not like ultra endurance racing.

1108
00:42:55.112 --> 00:42:57.746
You know you have one rider and then you have your team

1109
00:42:57.746 --> 00:43:00.144
that they're your domestic.

1110
00:43:00.144 --> 00:43:01.506
And they're protecting you from the wind,

1111
00:43:01.506 --> 00:43:05.643
they're getting you food, they're chasing things down.

1112
00:43:05.643 --> 00:43:08.628
So, I had to learn all of that.

1113
00:43:08.628 --> 00:43:10.802
And it's a process, I think for pro racers,

1114
00:43:10.802 --> 00:43:12.690
I think many people will agree with me that it takes

1115
00:43:12.690 --> 00:43:15.117
about five years before you actually start getting good

1116
00:43:15.117 --> 00:43:18.793
and start learning what you're good at.

1117
00:43:18.793 --> 00:43:20.427
And riding with the national team too,

1118
00:43:20.427 --> 00:43:22.978
they sent me to Europe which is night and day compared

1119
00:43:22.978 --> 00:43:26.116
to North America, like the level of racing.

1120
00:43:26.116 --> 00:43:29.240
So when I came back from Europe to North America,

1121
00:43:29.240 --> 00:43:32.338
I mean, my level had jumped two folds.

1122
00:43:32.338 --> 00:43:33.698
Sorry to interrupt the flow,

1123
00:43:33.698 --> 00:43:36.157
we'll be right back with more awesome but I wanna snag

1124
00:43:36.157 --> 00:43:40.657
a moment to talk to you about the importance of nutrition.

1125
00:43:40.657 --> 00:43:44.146
The thing is, most people I know actually already know

1126
00:43:44.146 --> 00:43:45.960
how to eat better and aspire

1127
00:43:45.960 --> 00:43:49.692
to incorporate more whole plants, more fruits, vegetables,

1128
00:43:49.692 --> 00:43:53.195
seeds, beans, and legumes into their daily routine.

1129
00:43:53.195 --> 00:43:55.586
Sadly however, without the kitchen tools

1130
00:43:55.586 --> 00:43:58.630
and support very few end up sticking with it.

1131
00:43:58.630 --> 00:44:03.277
So, because adopting a plant-based diet transformed my life

1132
00:44:03.277 --> 00:44:05.618
so profoundly and because I want everybody

1133
