1153
00:45:03.913 --> 00:45:06.653 line:15%
All right, let's get back to the show.

1154
00:45:06.653 --> 00:45:09.490
So, backtracking a little bit though,

1155
00:45:09.490 --> 00:45:12.011
at 38, you come back after the off season

1156
00:45:12.011 --> 00:45:14.163
and you're a completely different rider.

1157
00:45:14.163 --> 00:45:16.574
You're approaching it much more strategically,

1158
00:45:16.574 --> 00:45:18.174
you've got this climbing coach

1159
00:45:18.174 --> 00:45:20.726
and you have these breakthroughs.

1160
00:45:20.726 --> 00:45:24.212
I presume that then garners the notice

1161
00:45:24.212 --> 00:45:27.898
of these team directors and how do you kind of progress

1162
00:45:27.898 --> 00:45:29.386
through the ranks at that point?

1163
00:45:29.386 --> 00:45:31.210
Oh my God, at that point...

1164
00:45:31.210 --> 00:45:34.120
I mean, I could have gone to any team, you know what I mean?

1165
00:45:34.120 --> 00:45:35.148
They're like, what do you want?

1166
00:45:35.148 --> 00:45:36.280
The difference was that drastic?

1167
00:45:36.280 --> 00:45:37.203
It was that drastic.

1168
00:45:37.203 --> 00:45:39.103
I mean, even doing it as a one woman team

1169
00:45:39.103 --> 00:45:40.942
and showing them what I could do, right?

1170
00:45:40.942 --> 00:45:42.393
But I don't know if you know that

1171
00:45:42.393 --> 00:45:44.320
when I reached the peak kind of at that point

1172
00:45:44.320 --> 00:45:46.693
where opportunities were handed to me,

1173
00:45:46.693 --> 00:45:48.742
I had the mother of all crashes in Cascade

1174
00:45:48.742 --> 00:45:51.787
and where I wanted to kind of to prove myself

1175
00:45:51.787 --> 00:45:55.811
on a more international field where there were teams

1176
00:45:55.811 --> 00:45:57.929
from the Ukraine and Russia and you know,

1177
00:45:57.929 --> 00:46:00.626
Kristin Armstrong in the United States was there.

1178
00:46:00.626 --> 00:46:01.878
And so let's see if I could really climb

1179
00:46:01.878 --> 00:46:04.762
against the best riders in the world and so I went to

1180
00:46:04.762 --> 00:46:07.777
that particular race and I knew on the first,

1181
00:46:07.777 --> 00:46:09.587
that first stage there was a long climb

1182
00:46:09.587 --> 00:46:11.814
that they would take off and they took off and I could...

1183
00:46:11.814 --> 00:46:14.151
I was the only rider in that peloton and whatever

1184
00:46:14.151 --> 00:46:15.460
that could hold that kind of

1185
00:46:15.460 --> 00:46:17.546
that really fast paced up the climb.

1186
00:46:17.546 --> 00:46:19.371
But then you know, you start descending.

1187
00:46:19.371 --> 00:46:21.658
On those bikes, you can go very fast almost up to,

1188
00:46:21.658 --> 00:46:22.491
what is it?

1189
00:46:22.491 --> 00:46:25.269
100 kilometers an hour, 80 miles an hour.

1190
00:46:25.269 --> 00:46:27.535
And as we're descending, other riders are starting

1191
00:46:27.535 --> 00:46:30.486
to come up and we're, and I'm seeing like 85, 86.

1192
00:46:30.486 --> 00:46:34.460
And then there's a rider kinda coming on my left-hand side.

1193
00:46:34.460 --> 00:46:37.561
And there's a center line rule and she kinda leans into me

1194
00:46:37.561 --> 00:46:40.920
at 80 kilometers an hour, I landed on my face.

1195
00:46:40.920 --> 00:46:43.783
So I basically lips off, instant face lift.

1196
00:46:43.783 --> 00:46:45.295
Right, your whole face peeled off.

1197
00:46:45.295 --> 00:46:49.221
Yeah, I mean I broke almost 20 bones in my body.

1198
00:46:49.221 --> 00:46:52.654
The friction of the fall burned off the first layer of skin.

1199
00:46:52.654 --> 00:46:53.549
It was a nightmare.

1200
00:46:53.549 --> 00:46:55.370
I mean, I shouldn't be sitting here today based

1201
00:46:55.370 --> 00:46:56.658
on that crash, right?

1202
00:46:56.658 --> 00:46:58.825
So that was 2005, right?

1203
00:47:00.233 --> 00:47:03.349
Prior to that you had gone on this like tear winning

1204
00:47:03.349 --> 00:47:04.809
a bunch of races, right?

1205
00:47:04.809 --> 00:47:08.532
Like between, you won like nine out of 11 races leading up

1206
00:47:08.532 --> 00:47:11.067
to that Cascade Classic?

1207
00:47:11.067 --> 00:47:13.153
So you were in a pretty great position

1208
00:47:13.153 --> 00:47:17.662
in terms of really making a stamp on the pro circuit.

1209
00:47:17.662 --> 00:47:18.588
Oh, absolutely.

1210
00:47:18.588 --> 00:47:19.897
Although you'd had another,

1211
00:47:19.897 --> 00:47:22.187
you were in a position to make the 2004 Olympic team

1212
00:47:22.187 --> 00:47:23.689
but you had another crash, right?

1213
00:47:23.689 --> 00:47:25.797
That kinda took you out of contention for that.

1214
00:47:25.797 --> 00:47:26.660
Yeah.

1215
00:47:26.660 --> 00:47:29.095
And then you come back from that,

1216
00:47:29.095 --> 00:47:31.637
you don't make the Olympic team because of that crash.

1217
00:47:31.637 --> 00:47:33.161
But the Cascade Classic

1218
00:47:33.161 --> 00:47:35.257
is really gonna be this moment to shine.

1219
00:47:35.257 --> 00:47:37.455
Well, the Cascade Classic was kind of...

1220
00:47:37.455 --> 00:47:39.280
I mean, it was gonna be after that race

1221
00:47:39.280 --> 00:47:41.591
that I was gonna make my decision of, okay where am I gonna

1222
00:47:41.591 --> 00:47:44.451
like my breakthrough kinda riding career.

1223
00:47:44.451 --> 00:47:46.303
But being in that position,

1224
00:47:46.303 --> 00:47:49.077
I mean they basically told me that your career is over.

1225
00:47:49.077 --> 00:47:52.261
Your ability to walk properly without a walker

1226
00:47:52.261 --> 00:47:53.680
or cane will be very limited.

1227
00:47:53.680 --> 00:47:56.452
And then 100% you won't be back on your bike again.

1228
00:47:56.452 --> 00:47:58.277
And that's what I was left with as I was lying there

1229
00:47:58.277 --> 00:48:01.203
and you know in the trauma unit.

1230
00:48:01.203 --> 00:48:04.199
So, I believe, correct me if I'm wrong,

1231
00:48:04.199 --> 00:48:06.677
like Vail News dubbed this crash

1232
00:48:06.677 --> 00:48:09.112
like the most epic crash of all time, correct?

1233
00:48:09.112 --> 00:48:10.501
At that time, correct, yes.

1234
00:48:10.501 --> 00:48:11.463
Is it on film?

1235
00:48:11.463 --> 00:48:12.863
The crash?
Yeah.

1236
00:48:12.863 --> 00:48:15.050
No, I don't think so, I don't think they filmed the...

1237
00:48:15.050 --> 00:48:17.522
I mean, they did articles on it, right?

1238
00:48:17.522 --> 00:48:19.651
I mean we have pictures of the after a surgery stuff

1239
00:48:19.651 --> 00:48:21.486
but there is big write-ups on it

1240
00:48:21.486 --> 00:48:23.135
but I don't think it was filmed, no.

1241
00:48:23.135 --> 00:48:26.387
So you went down at like somewhere

1242
00:48:26.387 --> 00:48:28.488
between 60 and 80 miles an hour.

1243
00:48:28.488 --> 00:48:29.500
Correct, yeah.

1244
00:48:29.500 --> 00:48:31.544
On a descent.

1245
00:48:31.544 --> 00:48:33.259
With everybody else falling on top of me.

1246
00:48:33.259 --> 00:48:34.607
On your teeth, your lips.

1247
00:48:34.607 --> 00:48:36.320
I mean, you can't tell looking,

1248
00:48:36.320 --> 00:48:38.195
did you have to have like a lot of plastic surgery?

1249
00:48:38.195 --> 00:48:40.056
Oh yeah, I had lots of surgery done around.

1250
00:48:40.056 --> 00:48:43.764
I mean, I'm still kinda numb on my right side here.

1251
00:48:43.764 --> 00:48:45.788
There is a difference if you look at my younger pictures,

1252
00:48:45.788 --> 00:48:47.790
my face to what it is now there is...

1253
00:48:47.790 --> 00:48:52.506
I mean it's, there was a little bit of a difference.

1254
00:48:52.506 --> 00:48:55.197
Skin shorn off, your torso.

1255
00:48:55.197 --> 00:48:56.030
Yeah.

1256
00:48:56.030 --> 00:48:59.135
And you were told that you might not walk properly again.

1257
00:48:59.135 --> 00:49:00.648
Correct, I mean I broke my...

1258
00:49:00.648 --> 00:49:04.247
Both my ischium was shattered, my hips were broken, my arms.

1259
00:49:04.247 --> 00:49:07.461
I mean, fingers and toes busted, I was...

1260
00:49:07.461 --> 00:49:10.619
It's like taking a pretzel and basically stepping on it.

1261
00:49:10.619 --> 00:49:12.193
I can't even explain to you the worst pain

1262
00:49:12.193 --> 00:49:13.034
I'd ever been through.

1263
00:49:13.034 --> 00:49:15.007
Everything hurt all the time, breathing, blinking,

1264
00:49:15.007 --> 00:49:17.672
people opening and closing the door the wind from that.

1265
00:49:17.672 --> 00:49:19.911
It was a tough go.

1266
00:49:19.911 --> 00:49:22.125
Wow, how long were you in the hospital?

1267
00:49:22.125 --> 00:49:24.474
Well, they couldn't move me out of the United States

1268
00:49:24.474 --> 00:49:26.834
for I think four weeks.

1269
00:49:26.834 --> 00:49:28.648
And then they transferred me to...

1270
00:49:28.648 --> 00:49:30.361
'Cause I had to be airlifted from the crash

1271
00:49:30.361 --> 00:49:33.172
to St. Charles in Bend, Oregon.

1272
00:49:33.172 --> 00:49:36.724
And then my sister came to pick me up 'cause I couldn't...

1273
00:49:36.724 --> 00:49:39.067
It was gonna take a bit of process to drive me back

1274
00:49:39.067 --> 00:49:41.061
to Canada 'cause also getting expensive

1275
00:49:41.061 --> 00:49:44.564
'cause medical is expensive here, right?

1276
00:49:44.564 --> 00:49:47.463
So, months in the hospital.

1277
00:49:47.463 --> 00:49:48.296
Yes.

1278
00:49:48.296 --> 00:49:50.957
But you have the gift of these doctors telling you,

1279
00:49:50.957 --> 00:49:53.398
you're not gonna walk again let alone get on a bike

1280
00:49:53.398 --> 00:49:55.367
and ever race again, right?

1281
00:49:55.367 --> 00:49:58.620
So what do you do with that information?

1282
00:49:58.620 --> 00:50:00.503
You know what I said to them in my head,

1283
00:50:00.503 --> 00:50:02.395
I won't say it on air, gonna show you.

1284
00:50:02.395 --> 00:50:05.106
I mean, how do you begin the rehab process?

1285
00:50:05.106 --> 00:50:06.301
Well listen, the only thing

1286
00:50:06.301 --> 00:50:08.464
I could do was contract my abs.

1287
00:50:08.464 --> 00:50:11.205
That was the only physical thing I could do.

1288
00:50:11.205 --> 00:50:13.392
But I basically made a promise to myself and I said,

1289
00:50:13.392 --> 00:50:14.807
I don't care how long it takes

1290
00:50:14.807 --> 00:50:16.576
or the kind of pain I'm gonna go through,

1291
00:50:16.576 --> 00:50:18.486
I'm gonna get back on that bike, I'm gonna race again

1292
00:50:18.486 --> 00:50:20.097
and I'm gonna come back even stronger

1293
00:50:20.097 --> 00:50:22.802
than I did before all this happened.

1294
00:50:22.802 --> 00:50:25.843
And I think, it happened to a lot of people when your faced

1295
00:50:25.843 --> 00:50:27.745
with this overwhelming situations,

1296
00:50:27.745 --> 00:50:30.488
you don't know what to do so you kinda buckle.

1297
00:50:30.488 --> 00:50:32.374
So I knew I had a lot of work cut out for me

1298
00:50:32.374 --> 00:50:34.310
but I think everything is upstairs.

1299
00:50:34.310 --> 00:50:35.984
It's what you believe in, what you make your body believe

1300
00:50:35.984 --> 00:50:37.509
that you can do.

1301
00:50:37.509 --> 00:50:39.788
Because honestly, if I believed everything

1302
00:50:39.788 --> 00:50:41.777
that I was told even growing up,

1303
00:50:41.777 --> 00:50:43.911
then I wouldn't again, I wouldn't be sitting here today.

1304
00:50:43.911 --> 00:50:44.931
Right.

1305
00:50:44.931 --> 00:50:48.404
But we all have our breaking points too.

1306
00:50:48.404 --> 00:50:49.237
We do.

1307
00:50:49.237 --> 00:50:51.448
But your ability to kind of come compartmentalize all

1308
00:50:51.448 --> 00:50:55.305
of that and just focus on, okay I can do this.

1309
00:50:55.305 --> 00:50:59.467
Like how much of your military training comes into play

1310
00:50:59.467 --> 00:51:03.663
in terms of having the mental rigor to just block

1311
00:51:03.663 --> 00:51:07.246
out the fear and focus on the task at hand.

1312
00:51:08.306 --> 00:51:10.060
I think the military has a lot to do with it

1313
00:51:10.060 --> 00:51:11.815
because it was part of their job

1314
00:51:11.815 --> 00:51:14.825
even during my military train to crack us mentally

1315
00:51:14.825 --> 00:51:17.524
as best as they can, right?

1316
00:51:17.524 --> 00:51:19.297
'Cause mental strength is much more powerful

1317
00:51:19.297 --> 00:51:22.443
than physical strength, we all know that.

1318
00:51:22.443 --> 00:51:25.066
But for me, like at that point going back to the hospital,

1319
00:51:25.066 --> 00:51:27.096
I mean, how can they say something like that?

1320
00:51:27.096 --> 00:51:29.152
I haven't even started my rehab, you know what I mean?

1321
00:51:29.152 --> 00:51:32.258
Just to make that kind of prediction that fast.

1322
00:51:32.258 --> 00:51:34.988
And I think that's the thing is that we're so quick

1323
00:51:34.988 --> 00:51:39.887
to take the easy way out without being more optimistic

1324
00:51:39.887 --> 00:51:42.139
of the things that we possibly can do.

1325
00:51:42.139 --> 00:51:45.347
And the possibilities of just going after the things

1326
00:51:45.347 --> 00:51:46.626
that you believe in.

1327
00:51:46.626 --> 00:51:48.758
'Cause sometimes you're not gonna get support

1328
00:51:48.758 --> 00:51:51.433
from other people, you're gonna have to believe in yourself.

1329
00:51:51.433 --> 00:51:53.997
That's the thing is we're so influenced by other sources.

1330
00:51:53.997 --> 00:51:56.937
But for me, I never was, never has been, never will be.

1331
00:51:56.937 --> 00:51:58.809
What I wanna do and what I'm determined to do,

1332
00:51:58.809 --> 00:52:00.069
I'll make it happen.

1333
00:52:00.069 --> 00:52:02.684
I'll find a way to make it happen.

1334
00:52:02.684 --> 00:52:04.708
So make it happen you did.

1335
00:52:04.708 --> 00:52:07.138
Like how long was the rehabilitation

1336
00:52:07.138 --> 00:52:09.234
before you could get back on a bike?

1337
00:52:09.234 --> 00:52:13.996
I was back on my bike, race ready in less than a season.

1338
00:52:13.996 --> 00:52:15.267
So like-
Eight months.

1339
00:52:15.267 --> 00:52:16.709
Eight months, wow, wow.

1340
00:52:16.709 --> 00:52:19.512
And I was really skinny because I had my...

1341
00:52:19.512 --> 00:52:22.287
My lips had to be sown back on so I couldn't eat anything.

1342
00:52:22.287 --> 00:52:24.005
So I was climbing like a fin.

1343
00:52:24.005 --> 00:52:25.109
(Leah laughs)

1344
00:52:25.109 --> 00:52:28.019
So how long before you could walk properly

1345
00:52:28.019 --> 00:52:29.413
like take me through the stages of-

1346
00:52:29.413 --> 00:52:32.205
Well I mean, okay well, it was tough.

1347
00:52:32.205 --> 00:52:34.834
I mean I had a lot of rehab on my own,

1348
00:52:34.834 --> 00:52:36.653
I was in a wheelchair.

1349
00:52:36.653 --> 00:52:38.407
So I remember asking my mom to drop me off

1350
00:52:38.407 --> 00:52:39.656
at like a running track.

1351
00:52:39.656 --> 00:52:41.019
And I had one arm that I...

1352
00:52:41.019 --> 00:52:44.576
And my ankle, my left ankle, I can't move very well but...

1353
00:52:44.576 --> 00:52:46.768
And so I'd just wheel around the track as much as I can

1354
00:52:46.768 --> 00:52:48.775
and then I'd start using more, but mobility...

1355
00:52:48.775 --> 00:52:50.193
In a wheelchair.
Yeah, in a wheelchair.

1356
00:52:50.193 --> 00:52:51.651
But mobility was really important,

1357
00:52:51.651 --> 00:52:53.224
is getting those muscles strong again.

1358
00:52:53.224 --> 00:52:55.080
And it hurt like a son of a gun.

1359
00:52:55.080 --> 00:52:57.938
But every day I was better than the day before

1360
00:52:57.938 --> 00:53:00.277
and just staying positive and honesty,

1361
00:53:00.277 --> 00:53:02.593
it's sort of got being in that positive mode,

1362
00:53:02.593 --> 00:53:04.725
I could feel things starting to bind.

1363
00:53:04.725 --> 00:53:07.506
'Cause when I went to get re X-rayed at the general hospital

1364
00:53:07.506 --> 00:53:09.924
in Vancouver, a couple of months later,

1365
00:53:09.924 --> 00:53:13.512
they were floored at how fast I was recovering.

1366
00:53:13.512 --> 00:53:17.333
And you attribute that to just being in motion and-

1367
00:53:17.333 --> 00:53:19.520
I think in motion and mentally, you know what I mean.

1368
00:53:19.520 --> 00:53:21.533
And just determined that I am gonna get out of this.

1369
00:53:21.533 --> 00:53:22.632
Yeah.

1370
00:53:22.632 --> 00:53:26.089
So from the wheelchair then, like how do...

1371
00:53:26.089 --> 00:53:27.855
I mean, are you walking with a limp like how was-

1372
00:53:27.855 --> 00:53:30.985
Oh, yeah no, it goes from wheelchair to a one crutch.

1373
00:53:30.985 --> 00:53:31.935
You know what I mean?

1374
00:53:31.935 --> 00:53:33.652
Bopping away and then...

1375
00:53:33.652 --> 00:53:38.199
I mean, it was even a wheelchair to wheeling to my trainer

1376
00:53:38.199 --> 00:53:40.786
which was on a bike, whatever.

1377
00:53:40.786 --> 00:53:42.539
And it took me like 40 minutes just to get

1378
00:53:42.539 --> 00:53:44.635
from the wheelchair, just to sit and be comfortable

1379
00:53:44.635 --> 00:53:46.908
on the bike because it was the flashbacks.

1380
00:53:46.908 --> 00:53:50.132
Yeah, when you say you were race ready or back on the bike

1381
00:53:50.132 --> 00:53:52.987
and ready to race eight months later,

1382
00:53:52.987 --> 00:53:57.075
that doesn't account for the kind of mental trauma

1383
00:53:57.075 --> 00:54:01.027
of the recurring, like imagery of like crashing

1384
00:54:01.027 --> 00:54:02.924
at that kind of speed, right?
Absolutely, yeah.

1385
00:54:02.924 --> 00:54:05.447
And pro cycling, you have to be absolutely fearless.

1386
00:54:05.447 --> 00:54:06.670
Oh my gosh, yeah.

1387
00:54:06.670 --> 00:54:09.096
Like how do you ever descend aggressively again

1388
00:54:09.096 --> 00:54:10.300
after that experience?

1389
00:54:10.300 --> 00:54:11.321
Far away from the peloton.

1390
00:54:11.321 --> 00:54:12.707
How could you ever overcome that?

1391
00:54:12.707 --> 00:54:15.775
Yeah, like there's no way 'cause the edge that you have

1392
00:54:15.775 --> 00:54:19.295
to ride in order to be competitive at the elite level

1393
00:54:19.295 --> 00:54:21.998
is to really embrace that fearlessness.

1394
00:54:21.998 --> 00:54:24.484
And that's funny because that's why a lot of the races

1395
00:54:24.484 --> 00:54:26.050
I did, I tried to get into breakaways

1396
00:54:26.050 --> 00:54:28.447
where I wasn't with like early before.

1397
00:54:28.447 --> 00:54:30.215
Just right off the front at the beginning.

1398
00:54:30.215 --> 00:54:31.862
Or on the climbs, just take off so I don't have

1399
00:54:31.862 --> 00:54:33.117
to descend with anyone.

1400
00:54:33.117 --> 00:54:34.562
That might work like one out

1401
00:54:34.562 --> 00:54:36.852
of every 20 races but most of the time,

1402
00:54:36.852 --> 00:54:40.048
then you end up getting completely destroyed.

1403
00:54:40.048 --> 00:54:41.203
Yeah, I mean for sure.

1404
00:54:41.203 --> 00:54:42.162
I mean that was the hardest thing.

1405
00:54:42.162 --> 00:54:46.052
I mean with ascending, climbing was easy for me, right?

1406
00:54:46.052 --> 00:54:47.612
It was the descending that was really...

1407
00:54:47.612 --> 00:54:50.621
I mean I'd be breaking all the time and my climbing coach

1408
00:54:50.621 --> 00:54:53.365
that I hired earlier to make me this great climber,

1409
00:54:53.365 --> 00:54:56.069
he was now coaching me to descend without being...

1410
00:54:56.069 --> 00:54:57.652
Without having this fear because you know

1411
00:54:57.652 --> 00:54:59.158
you can't race having that kind of fear.

1412
00:54:59.158 --> 00:55:00.951
Right.
And there is a risk.

1413
00:55:00.951 --> 00:55:04.368
And with that fear, you become a hazard

1414
00:55:05.568 --> 00:55:06.660
to the other riders.

1415
00:55:06.660 --> 00:55:07.805
Absolutely yeah.

1416
00:55:07.805 --> 00:55:10.664
But mind you too, I mean, I was kinda like...

1417
00:55:10.664 --> 00:55:12.381
When we were descending, I would kind of veer off

1418
00:55:12.381 --> 00:55:15.638
to the side a little bit more, give myself more space.

1419
00:55:15.638 --> 00:55:19.163
Try to stay to the front as much as possible.

1420
00:55:19.163 --> 00:55:21.505
And I realized too like after that crash,

1421
00:55:21.505 --> 00:55:23.576
I mean I had great years the next three years after

1422
00:55:23.576 --> 00:55:26.886
but I couldn't afford to crash 'cause you know,

1423
00:55:26.886 --> 00:55:28.316
I think two years after that I was hit

1424
00:55:28.316 --> 00:55:29.535
by the car in Redlands.

1425
00:55:29.535 --> 00:55:30.848
Oh, I don't know about that.

1426
00:55:30.848 --> 00:55:33.138
Yeah, it's actually one of my,

1427
00:55:33.138 --> 00:55:36.047
close to my last year of racing in a criterion was

1428
00:55:36.047 --> 00:55:38.816
at Redland stage race and we were warming up for the crit

1429
00:55:38.816 --> 00:55:40.298
and I was coming back to the team car

1430
00:55:40.298 --> 00:55:42.587
and this lady in a Hyundai who was texting,

1431
00:55:42.587 --> 00:55:45.383
she was going like 80 kilometers an hour, 50 miles an hour

1432
00:55:45.383 --> 00:55:47.149
or whatever, she hits me from the rear of the bike

1433
00:55:47.149 --> 00:55:50.775
and ejected me off my bike and I landed like Superman

1434
00:55:50.775 --> 00:55:53.039
onto the pavement and stabbed both my hands,

1435
00:55:53.039 --> 00:55:55.376
I had two compound fractures.

1436
00:55:55.376 --> 00:55:57.048
You know like, gosh, here we go again. (Leah laughs)

1437
00:55:57.048 --> 00:56:01.169
So what are you like at this point 40 something?

1438
00:56:01.169 --> 00:56:02.752
I'm now 42, yeah.

1439
00:56:03.821 --> 00:56:06.473
And so I had retired that, I said, I can't do this anymore.

1440
00:56:06.473 --> 00:56:08.591
Were you the oldest rider on the peloton?

1441
00:56:08.591 --> 00:56:12.669
No, no Jeannie Longo, he's the most decorated athlete,

1442
00:56:12.669 --> 00:56:14.353
she was older.

1443
00:56:14.353 --> 00:56:16.342
Linda Jackson was in there too.

1444
00:56:16.342 --> 00:56:17.175
So there was more.

1445
00:56:17.175 --> 00:56:18.873
I mean, I think women pushed the envelope a little bit more

1446
00:56:18.873 --> 00:56:21.684
than the men with racing.

1447
00:56:21.684 --> 00:56:22.905
So, but I was...

1448
00:56:22.905 --> 00:56:24.842
I mean, I had planned to kinda transition

1449
00:56:24.842 --> 00:56:28.210
into ultra-endurance racing during that time

1450
00:56:28.210 --> 00:56:30.795
but that was just, yeah, the accident was-

1451
00:56:30.795 --> 00:56:32.898
Because no crazy descents.

1452
00:56:32.898 --> 00:56:36.727
I know I have the mental fortitude for something like this,

1453
00:56:36.727 --> 00:56:38.556
I don't crack easily.

1454
00:56:38.556 --> 00:56:41.192
Like this could be something to pursue.

1455
00:56:41.192 --> 00:56:43.559
While you're racing you're starting to think about that?

1456
00:56:43.559 --> 00:56:46.198
Well, I mean I think the reality is that I had crushed.

1457
00:56:46.198 --> 00:56:47.970
That's not the only crash you have,

1458
00:56:47.970 --> 00:56:49.854
when you're a pro racer, you crashed multiple times.

1459
00:56:49.854 --> 00:56:52.863
I broke my back in France when I was there

1460
00:56:52.863 --> 00:56:56.242
and multiple crashes where I cracked my helmet, right?

1461
00:56:56.242 --> 00:56:59.567
I mean, I think to the safety of me

1462
00:56:59.567 --> 00:57:01.655
like there's life after cycling.

1463
00:57:01.655 --> 00:57:04.270
It just felt like a right time to transition

1464
00:57:04.270 --> 00:57:06.711
'cause I really wanted to do ultra endurance racing,

1465
00:57:06.711 --> 00:57:10.265
which is a lot safer in that regards.

1466
00:57:10.265 --> 00:57:13.719
And so, just I didn't make it there without that last.

1467
00:57:13.719 --> 00:57:18.661
So after that crash or where the car hit you at Redlands,

1468
00:57:18.661 --> 00:57:20.767
that was like the final moment, like I'm done.

1469
00:57:20.767 --> 00:57:21.600
Correct, yes.

1470
00:57:21.600 --> 00:57:23.618
Done being a pro racer.
Yeah.

1471
00:57:23.618 --> 00:57:24.779
So what happens?

1472
00:57:24.779 --> 00:57:26.624
You go back to Canada and start

1473
00:57:26.624 --> 00:57:28.469
to hatch some plan to become?

1474
00:57:28.469 --> 00:57:30.510
Well, the plan was Race Across America.

1475
00:57:30.510 --> 00:57:31.782
I mean I remember watching that and saying,

1476
00:57:31.782 --> 00:57:35.555
I'm gonna do that before I retire completely from cycling.

1477
00:57:35.555 --> 00:57:39.456
'Cause the goal was, I mean, I just felt like I would...

1478
00:57:39.456 --> 00:57:40.483
I was be born gifted,

1479
00:57:40.483 --> 00:57:42.796
I was more gifted for ultra endurance racing based

1480
00:57:42.796 --> 00:57:46.419
on my ability to ignore pain, push myself beyond my limits

1481
00:57:46.419 --> 00:57:49.648
and to be functionable with very little sleep.

1482
00:57:49.648 --> 00:57:50.972
That's military training.

1483
00:57:50.972 --> 00:57:53.415 line:15%
Yeah, yeah, walk me through the military training

1484
00:57:53.415 --> 00:57:55.019 line:15%
around sleep deprivation 'cause this is

1485
00:57:55.019 --> 00:57:56.935 line:15%
like a superpower that you have.

1486
00:57:56.935 --> 00:57:59.252 line:15%
And I'm sort of the mind

1487
00:57:59.252 --> 00:58:02.282 line:15%
that you can develop mental fortitude around it

1488
00:58:02.282 --> 00:58:06.596
but it's also not something you can you truly train it?

1489
00:58:06.596 --> 00:58:10.238
Like in my experience 'cause I've tried to practice it,

1490
00:58:10.238 --> 00:58:11.406
and then when you get to the event

1491
00:58:11.406 --> 00:58:13.524
and you need to rely on it, it doesn't really work.

1492
00:58:13.524 --> 00:58:14.357
Yeah.

1493
00:58:14.357 --> 00:58:16.113
Like practicing it doesn't make it any easier.

1494
00:58:16.113 --> 00:58:18.464
No, I think from what I understand

1495
00:58:18.464 --> 00:58:20.347
that either you have it or you don't.

1496
00:58:20.347 --> 00:58:21.791
That you can't, there's something you can't train.

1497
00:58:21.791 --> 00:58:24.214
I don't know, that's based on some research.

1498
00:58:24.214 --> 00:58:25.971
There're some people who are just constitutionally wired

1499
00:58:25.971 --> 00:58:28.669
to be like, when you hear these legendary stories

1500
00:58:28.669 --> 00:58:30.748
of some of the RAAM racers over the years,

1501
00:58:30.748 --> 00:58:33.076
like your aerobic and all these people

1502
00:58:33.076 --> 00:58:35.133
who could literally just sleep an hour a night

1503
00:58:35.133 --> 00:58:38.355
and seem to churn it out no problemo.

1504
00:58:38.355 --> 00:58:41.283
It's not no problemo but it comes with challenges, right?

1505
00:58:41.283 --> 00:58:44.309
Like you hallucinate really bad and stuff.

1506
00:58:44.309 --> 00:58:47.622
But yeah your body, you just become like a potato,

1507
00:58:47.622 --> 00:58:49.222
you don't know what's real what's not real

1508
00:58:49.222 --> 00:58:51.474
like a lot of time with sleep deprivation during

1509
00:58:51.474 --> 00:58:52.546
a Race Across America, I didn't know

1510
00:58:52.546 --> 00:58:53.949
what I was doing on the bike.

1511
00:58:53.949 --> 00:58:57.081
Because I too, we too have my sleep to 90 minutes.

1512
00:58:57.081 --> 00:58:58.602
But you had experience with that.

1513
00:58:58.602 --> 00:59:00.956
Like in military training, they're probably waking you up

1514
00:59:00.956 --> 00:59:02.375
in the middle of the night all the time.

1515
00:59:02.375 --> 00:59:04.727
Oh, they're not even waking up, you're awake.

1516
00:59:04.727 --> 00:59:05.821
(both laughs)

1517
00:59:05.821 --> 00:59:08.191
There is no I'm waking you up.

1518
00:59:08.191 --> 00:59:09.024
I mean-

1519
00:59:09.024 --> 00:59:10.285
But mind games, there's a lot of mind games.

1520
00:59:10.285 --> 00:59:11.592
Oh totally, oh yeah.

1521
00:59:11.592 --> 00:59:14.622
I mean, I remember once training, we had a really hard trek,

1522
00:59:14.622 --> 00:59:16.356
it was through the night.

1523
00:59:16.356 --> 00:59:19.057
We finished the trek after I think nine or 10 hours,

1524
00:59:19.057 --> 00:59:21.643
we come back to our base or barracks or whatever.

1525
00:59:21.643 --> 00:59:23.076
The commander says, you guys can rest,

1526
00:59:23.076 --> 00:59:25.382
we'll give you four hours and then we're back at it, right?

1527
00:59:25.382 --> 00:59:27.169
So we're all, thank God and I'm thinking you know what,

1528
00:59:27.169 --> 00:59:28.887
this is too good to be true.

1529
00:59:28.887 --> 00:59:30.895
If he says that, you know that's not gonna be there.

1530
00:59:30.895 --> 00:59:33.764
And so she leaves and then 30 seconds later,

1531
00:59:33.764 --> 00:59:35.519
another guy comes in, opens the lights,

1532
00:59:35.519 --> 00:59:36.400
he says get your stuff back on,

1533
00:59:36.400 --> 00:59:37.871
we're heading back out again.

1534
00:59:37.871 --> 00:59:38.704
Right.

1535
00:59:38.704 --> 00:59:39.799
So that's the kind of stuff that they,

1536
00:59:39.799 --> 00:59:40.682
you know what I mean?

1537
00:59:40.682 --> 00:59:41.620
And people are crying and screaming and-

1538
00:59:41.620 --> 00:59:43.387
That's very instructive for ultra endurance.

1539
00:59:43.387 --> 00:59:44.300
Yeah, exactly.

1540
00:59:44.300 --> 00:59:45.519
So, that was called.

1541
00:59:45.519 --> 00:59:47.111
I mean, if something sounds too good to be true,

1542
00:59:47.111 --> 00:59:48.567
you know it's not gonna happen.

1543
00:59:48.567 --> 00:59:49.488
Right.
Yeah.

1544
00:59:49.488 --> 00:59:51.850
So you're going into this idea around RAAM

1545
00:59:51.850 --> 00:59:54.192
with a very specific set of skills.

1546
00:59:54.192 --> 00:59:55.559
I mean, there are other...

1547
00:59:55.559 --> 00:59:57.064
Like some of these Eastern European guys

1548
00:59:57.064 --> 00:59:59.675
who've raced RAAM I think are military officers, right?

1549
00:59:59.675 --> 01:00:00.508
Correct.

1550
01:00:00.508 --> 01:00:02.412
Some of the great guys over the years.

1551
01:00:02.412 --> 01:00:05.948
So it's not a new thing that somebody with your discipline

1552
01:00:05.948 --> 01:00:08.107
and background would be interested in this

1553
01:00:08.107 --> 01:00:09.653
but you are coming into it

1554
01:00:09.653 --> 01:00:14.347
with this pro cycling career plus the military training.

1555
01:00:14.347 --> 01:00:16.606
I mean, you're positioned well for it.

1556
01:00:16.606 --> 01:00:18.901
I'm positioning well, mentally for it absolutely, right?

1557
01:00:18.901 --> 01:00:21.228
You know, I mean I think that has a lot...

1558
01:00:21.228 --> 01:00:22.425
The benefits of that.

1559
01:00:22.425 --> 01:00:23.738
But even in pro racing,

1560
01:00:23.738 --> 01:00:28.108
I mean I was better in longer races, I got stronger later.

1561
01:00:28.108 --> 01:00:30.321
Again, I could stay awake.

1562
01:00:30.321 --> 01:00:33.510
And so I just thought things that were extended and longer,

1563
01:00:33.510 --> 01:00:35.537
my ability to recover really fast too

1564
01:00:35.537 --> 01:00:37.084
was another asset that I had.

1565
01:00:37.084 --> 01:00:39.865
So, I just felt like it was more suited for me again,

1566
01:00:39.865 --> 01:00:40.997
ultra endurance racing.

1567
01:00:40.997 --> 01:00:43.234
Yeah, so the first time you do...

1568
01:00:43.234 --> 01:00:45.158
You've done RAAM three times, right?

1569
01:00:45.158 --> 01:00:46.221
So what was...

1570
01:00:46.221 --> 01:00:48.407
The first year was 2011?

1571
01:00:48.407 --> 01:00:49.694
Correct.
Yeah.

1572
01:00:49.694 --> 01:00:53.480
So talk to me about how you transitioned your training

1573
01:00:53.480 --> 01:00:54.688
to prepare for that.

1574
01:00:54.688 --> 01:00:56.288
Well, I mean-
It's a very different thing.

1575
01:00:56.288 --> 01:00:57.691
I didn't know what I was doing.

1576
01:00:57.691 --> 01:00:59.499
I didn't hire a coach or whatever.

1577
01:00:59.499 --> 01:01:03.205
So I just knew I had to stay on my bike and ride it nonstop.

1578
01:01:03.205 --> 01:01:05.431
And so what I did is I just self-trained

1579
01:01:05.431 --> 01:01:08.263
and even my crew, none of us did it before.

1580
01:01:08.263 --> 01:01:09.849
We didn't know what we were getting into, right?

1581
01:01:09.849 --> 01:01:11.671
So I would train multiple...

1582
01:01:11.671 --> 01:01:13.503
Like at 3:00 in the morning or 6:00 in the morning

1583
01:01:13.503 --> 01:01:15.848
or 12:00 at night so you know,

1584
01:01:15.848 --> 01:01:18.814
just to train my body and I thought I knew I was doing it

1585
01:01:18.814 --> 01:01:20.392
and I basically come to the line.

1586
01:01:20.392 --> 01:01:22.296
At that point, I thought in the best shape

1587
01:01:22.296 --> 01:01:23.194
of my life, right?

1588
01:01:23.194 --> 01:01:25.028
But Race Across America is a different animal.

1589
01:01:25.028 --> 01:01:26.781
It's something you can't...

1590
01:01:26.781 --> 01:01:29.096
It's really hard to describe it in words.

1591
01:01:29.096 --> 01:01:31.397
'Cause remember you have 12 days to get your body

1592
01:01:31.397 --> 01:01:32.266
across the country.

1593
01:01:32.266 --> 01:01:34.789
So, a lot of things happen during that time.

1594
01:01:34.789 --> 01:01:36.842
It's not a matter of if but when.

1595
01:01:36.842 --> 01:01:38.442
You know you have back neck, knee, constipation,

1596
01:01:38.442 --> 01:01:40.748
diarrhea, swelling, and you have to have a crew

1597
01:01:40.748 --> 01:01:43.108
that knows what they're doing navigational wise,

1598
01:01:43.108 --> 01:01:45.226
keeping you safe, knowing the terrain,

1599
01:01:45.226 --> 01:01:47.200
knowing when there's a bike change.

1600
01:01:47.200 --> 01:01:50.021
So, we kinda went in there completely green.

1601
01:01:50.021 --> 01:01:50.877
Yeah.

1602
01:01:50.877 --> 01:01:53.514
We should probably explain for people that are listening

1603
01:01:53.514 --> 01:01:55.514
or watching who don't know what RAAM is.

1604
01:01:55.514 --> 01:01:59.847
RAAM is a cycling race that began like 40 years ago.

1605
01:02:01.181 --> 01:02:03.140
Yeah, 39 years ago, correct.

1606
01:02:03.140 --> 01:02:06.208
That entails riding your bike across the country

1607
01:02:06.208 --> 01:02:08.305
on a predesignated route.

1608
01:02:08.305 --> 01:02:11.993
You begin in Oceanside, California, you end up in Annapolis.

1609
01:02:11.993 --> 01:02:13.361
Is it the same route every year?

1610
01:02:13.361 --> 01:02:14.613
Do they ever switch it up at all?

1611
01:02:14.613 --> 01:02:16.176
Now, it has the last...

1612
01:02:16.176 --> 01:02:17.127
They do switch up.

1613
01:02:17.127 --> 01:02:19.548
Like the first RAAM was, I started up Northmore,

1614
01:02:19.548 --> 01:02:20.788
it went through a different terrain

1615
01:02:20.788 --> 01:02:23.297
but a little bit shorter, less climbing.

1616
01:02:23.297 --> 01:02:24.972
But the last I'm gonna say 10 years,

1617
01:02:24.972 --> 01:02:26.194
it's been the Annapolis.

1618
01:02:26.194 --> 01:02:27.920
The same route, right?
Yeah.

1619
01:02:27.920 --> 01:02:30.790
There's a solo division where people like yourselves,

1620
01:02:30.790 --> 01:02:32.624
just do it the way they wanna do it

1621
01:02:32.624 --> 01:02:35.322
and then there are relay divisions which is different.

1622
01:02:35.322 --> 01:02:37.196
And that's super interesting too and the way

1623
01:02:37.196 --> 01:02:41.546
that the relays decide how to configure their rotation,

1624
01:02:41.546 --> 01:02:43.698
whether they're gonna do six hour shifts

1625
01:02:43.698 --> 01:02:46.243
or 30 minute shifts, it's super fascinating.

1626
01:02:46.243 --> 01:02:48.467
But in any of that, the idea is you ride your bike

1627
01:02:48.467 --> 01:02:51.899
across the country and you can sleep as much as you want

1628
01:02:51.899 --> 01:02:54.342
or you can sleep not at all, it's completely up to you.

1629
01:02:54.342 --> 01:02:57.308
You're supported by a crew that follows you,

1630
01:02:57.308 --> 01:02:58.917
that supports you, that feeds you

1631
01:02:58.917 --> 01:03:03.095
and that's gotta be an unbelievably rigorous job.

1632
01:03:03.095 --> 01:03:05.710
And there are legendary stories of people

1633
01:03:05.710 --> 01:03:09.438
who have attempted this, who have succeeded at doing this.

1634
01:03:09.438 --> 01:03:10.355
And it's...

1635
01:03:14.690 --> 01:03:17.366
There are tales of woe and tears

1636
01:03:17.366 --> 01:03:18.890
and hallucinations and cry.

1637
01:03:18.890 --> 01:03:21.165
I mean like the drama is unbelievable.

1638
01:03:21.165 --> 01:03:22.126
Oh yeah.

1639
01:03:22.126 --> 01:03:23.222
Even with, and don't forget too,

1640
01:03:23.222 --> 01:03:24.886
you've got 12 days to do it.

1641
01:03:24.886 --> 01:03:26.211
So when you say you can sleep as much

1642
01:03:26.211 --> 01:03:28.109
as you want not necessarily, right.

1643
01:03:28.109 --> 01:03:29.070
Because if you sleep-

1644
01:03:29.070 --> 01:03:30.467
12 days ain't that much.

1645
01:03:30.467 --> 01:03:32.240
Yeah, I mean in a 48 hour period,

1646
01:03:32.240 --> 01:03:34.451
it's either zero to three hours but most people,

1647
01:03:34.451 --> 01:03:36.593
every like, you always go through the first start

1648
01:03:36.593 --> 01:03:39.714
so you're gonna ride about the first 40 hours nonstop.

1649
01:03:39.714 --> 01:03:43.010
And then you'll go into your sleep cycle of every 24 hours

1650
01:03:43.010 --> 01:03:45.344
from like 90 minutes to three hours

1651
01:03:45.344 --> 01:03:47.185
I'm gonna say it's usually the time.

1652
01:03:47.185 --> 01:03:49.943
And your sleep schedule evolved through the experience

1653
01:03:49.943 --> 01:03:52.260
of these three races before you kinda figured out

1654
01:03:52.260 --> 01:03:53.634
what works best for you.

1655
01:03:53.634 --> 01:03:54.948
Yeah, I mean you learn something on.

1656
01:03:54.948 --> 01:03:56.694
I mean, that was a total learning curve

1657
01:03:56.694 --> 01:03:57.939
the first time, right.

1658
01:03:57.939 --> 01:04:00.475
You know also I got the condition of shermers neck.

1659
01:04:00.475 --> 01:04:03.376
Yeah, we're gonna spend a lot of time talking about that.

1660
01:04:03.376 --> 01:04:04.832
That was even more of a challenge.

1661
01:04:04.832 --> 01:04:08.836
So, that's the thing like the neck gives out

1662
01:04:08.836 --> 01:04:12.079
'cause you can't hold your eight to 10 pound head up

1663
01:04:12.079 --> 01:04:13.835
in that position for that amount

1664
01:04:13.835 --> 01:04:17.178
of time without it giving out and people's necks

1665
01:04:17.178 --> 01:04:19.234
just can't sustain their heads

1666
01:04:19.234 --> 01:04:23.121
and their heads end up resting on their hands basically

1667
01:04:23.121 --> 01:04:25.704
on the handlebars and it becomes a very perilous.

1668
01:04:25.704 --> 01:04:26.537
I mean, yeah.

1669
01:04:26.537 --> 01:04:28.345
I mean I didn't even know what Shermer's neck was.

1670
01:04:28.345 --> 01:04:31.970
I mean, when we started Race Across America in 2011...

1671
01:04:31.970 --> 01:04:34.044
I mean, I basically started the race like a jackass,

1672
01:04:34.044 --> 01:04:35.576
I went out through like a bat out of hell,

1673
01:04:35.576 --> 01:04:37.372
riding like I was a pro racer, right.

1674
01:04:37.372 --> 01:04:38.621
And I was good for three days.

1675
01:04:38.621 --> 01:04:42.024
Just blasted off through the desert in California.

1676
01:04:42.024 --> 01:04:44.235
And on day like two and a half, I thought okay,

1677
01:04:44.235 --> 01:04:46.888
I was on record breaking pace, I can sustain this is hard

1678
01:04:46.888 --> 01:04:47.899
but everything's good.

1679
01:04:47.899 --> 01:04:49.929
But then eventually, and shermers that happens,

1680
01:04:49.929 --> 01:04:53.349
it's like a slow onset, it's fast, right.

1681
01:04:53.349 --> 01:04:54.182
Then all of a sudden,

1682
01:04:54.182 --> 01:04:56.408
I was having a hard time keeping my head up,

1683
01:04:56.408 --> 01:05:01.408
'cause our neck isn't positioned, built to hold our head up,

1684
01:05:02.764 --> 01:05:04.869
it's there to support it, not to hold it.

1685
01:05:04.869 --> 01:05:08.118
So, I couldn't turn my head anymore

1686
01:05:08.118 --> 01:05:09.899
and then all of a sudden within three hours

1687
01:05:09.899 --> 01:05:12.226
of the first pain that kinda shut down my back

1688
01:05:12.226 --> 01:05:13.933
into my buttocks area.

1689
01:05:13.933 --> 01:05:15.728
My head completely dropped so my chin

1690
01:05:15.728 --> 01:05:17.272
is now resting on my chest.

1691
01:05:17.272 --> 01:05:18.230
You can't lift your head.

1692
01:05:18.230 --> 01:05:19.502
I cannot, I cannot lift it.

1693
01:05:19.502 --> 01:05:21.417
How many days in were you?

1694
01:05:21.417 --> 01:05:23.618
Two and a half days.

1695
01:05:23.618 --> 01:05:26.174
And so in order to ride the bike now, like you said,

1696
01:05:26.174 --> 01:05:29.187
I'm holding my head in my chin under the palm of my hand,

1697
01:05:29.187 --> 01:05:31.737
the other arm is steering braking and trying

1698
01:05:31.737 --> 01:05:32.892
to control the bike.

1699
01:05:32.892 --> 01:05:36.212
And so my crew members are saying, oh my God,

1700
01:05:36.212 --> 01:05:38.261
we're not even a quarter of the way into the race,

1701
01:05:38.261 --> 01:05:40.813
we have to pull out, you can't ride across the country.

1702
01:05:40.813 --> 01:05:42.076
And I remember saying you know what,

1703
01:05:42.076 --> 01:05:43.389
I've never quit anything in my life,

1704
01:05:43.389 --> 01:05:44.977
I don't care if I have to crawl across,

1705
01:05:44.977 --> 01:05:48.067
come up with something, we're gonna make it across.

1706
01:05:48.067 --> 01:05:50.136
So my crew is creative, they're really creative.

1707
01:05:50.136 --> 01:05:51.672
They came up with different apparatuses,

1708
01:05:51.672 --> 01:05:54.351
one was like a PVC pipe thing whatever

1709
01:05:54.351 --> 01:05:55.483
with a sling underneath.

1710
01:05:55.483 --> 01:05:57.041
I've seen lots of videos of that.

1711
01:05:57.041 --> 01:05:59.305
You have this PVC pipe going down your back

1712
01:05:59.305 --> 01:06:01.980
and then you literally like tape it to your forehead.

1713
01:06:01.980 --> 01:06:04.498
You look like you just got out of the spinal ward

1714
01:06:04.498 --> 01:06:07.070
at the hospital, it's insane.

1715
01:06:07.070 --> 01:06:08.825
Yeah. But none of those...

1716
01:06:08.825 --> 01:06:11.451
Another one was a big like an arm coming off your handlebars

1717
01:06:11.451 --> 01:06:14.066
like that you rest your chin on.

1718
01:06:14.066 --> 01:06:15.482
That seemed dangerous.

1719
01:06:15.482 --> 01:06:19.092
What worked for me that I still use in all my rounds

1720
01:06:19.092 --> 01:06:20.458
is gonna sound kinda crazy.

1721
01:06:20.458 --> 01:06:25.186
So, in every round, basically shaved my ear from ear to ear.

1722
01:06:25.186 --> 01:06:26.953
My hair is a little bit short here.

1723
01:06:26.953 --> 01:06:29.036
And then with the remainder of hair that's on the top

1724
01:06:29.036 --> 01:06:32.167
of my head, my crew chief will take tensor bandage,

1725
01:06:32.167 --> 01:06:34.235
she'll french braid it into three sections

1726
01:06:34.235 --> 01:06:35.182
on the top of my head.

1727
01:06:35.182 --> 01:06:37.150
She'll pull my head back and she'll tie it to the back

1728
01:06:37.150 --> 01:06:38.736
of my heart rate monitor.

1729
01:06:38.736 --> 01:06:41.714
It's kinda like a bubble we have whatever.

1730
01:06:41.714 --> 01:06:42.907
In the later races,

1731
01:06:42.907 --> 01:06:45.578
you actually wove cloth into those braids

1732
01:06:45.578 --> 01:06:46.959
so you could pull it down.

1733
01:06:46.959 --> 01:06:47.792
Correct, yeah.

1734
01:06:47.792 --> 01:06:49.676
And then you anchor it to your heart rate monitor,

1735
01:06:49.676 --> 01:06:50.791
strap on the back.

1736
01:06:50.791 --> 01:06:52.229
Yup, yup.

1737
01:06:52.229 --> 01:06:53.321
But that strap is,

1738
01:06:53.321 --> 01:06:55.155
I mean that strap would have to be pretty tight, right?

1739
01:06:55.155 --> 01:06:56.247
It's pretty tight.

1740
01:06:56.247 --> 01:06:58.396
But I start with that now too, I don't wait

1741
01:06:58.396 --> 01:07:00.436
until it gets really bad because I think they said

1742
01:07:00.436 --> 01:07:02.584
that shermer is actually really prone to people

1743
01:07:02.584 --> 01:07:05.116
who have suffered from whiplash or injury crashes.

1744
01:07:05.116 --> 01:07:07.668
So, and it's usually male not a lot of women.

1745
01:07:07.668 --> 01:07:10.298
So I think from the crash that I've had in the past.

1746
01:07:10.298 --> 01:07:12.801
And yeah, that's how I got across the country.

1747
01:07:12.801 --> 01:07:15.743
I mean, lemme tell you, it was the worst pain

1748
01:07:15.743 --> 01:07:18.054
I'd ever suffered through because your muscles

1749
01:07:18.054 --> 01:07:19.536
are so tight and it's so tense.

1750
01:07:19.536 --> 01:07:22.386
I mean, we had to rotate Aleve, Tylenol and Advil

1751
01:07:22.386 --> 01:07:24.752
just to control the pain, I'd be throwing up off the side

1752
01:07:24.752 --> 01:07:26.572
of the bike because of the pain.

1753
01:07:26.572 --> 01:07:27.972
It was excruciating.

1754
01:07:27.972 --> 01:07:31.600
But then with the weird contraption that you have,

1755
01:07:31.600 --> 01:07:33.522
does it feel like your hair is getting pulled out

1756
01:07:33.522 --> 01:07:34.593
of your head the whole time.
Absolutely,

1757
01:07:34.593 --> 01:07:36.127
it feels like it, they'll tell you to go run,

1758
01:07:36.127 --> 01:07:38.909
I'll grab you by the hair but you keep running, right.

1759
01:07:38.909 --> 01:07:41.143
That's what it feels like but I mean,

1760
01:07:41.143 --> 01:07:42.121
what else are you gonna do?

1761
01:07:42.121 --> 01:07:43.317
Nothing else worked, right.

1762
01:07:43.317 --> 01:07:45.713
And otherwise I can't see the road.

1763
01:07:45.713 --> 01:07:49.796
So that's either I get my hair pulled or I crash.

1764
01:07:50.912 --> 01:07:54.655
So 2011, you figure it out and you finish it.

1765
01:07:54.655 --> 01:07:56.451
But that was really just an education

1766
01:07:56.451 --> 01:07:58.203
in what this experience was all about.

1767
01:07:58.203 --> 01:08:01.066
I mean, I'm still able to, I still won the race.

1768
01:08:01.066 --> 01:08:04.152
I mean, I didn't set any records or anything.

1769
01:08:04.152 --> 01:08:04.985
And then I-

1770
01:08:04.985 --> 01:08:06.715
You won the women's female division.

1771
01:08:06.715 --> 01:08:08.469
Correct, yes.
Solo.

1772
01:08:08.469 --> 01:08:11.654
And then at that point I was done living out of my bags,

1773
01:08:11.654 --> 01:08:13.499
as you know I was pro racing for so many years,

1774
01:08:13.499 --> 01:08:17.217
then RAAM was kind of, and I was just ready to take a break.

1775
01:08:17.217 --> 01:08:18.103
So I actually-

1776
01:08:18.103 --> 01:08:18.936
How dare you?

1777
01:08:18.936 --> 01:08:20.013
Yeah, I know, I know.

1778
01:08:20.013 --> 01:08:21.647
You hadn't taken a break your whole life.

1779
01:08:21.647 --> 01:08:22.480
(Leah laughs)

1780
01:08:22.480 --> 01:08:26.308
You like 40, were you 42, 43 at this point?

1781
01:08:26.308 --> 01:08:28.801
Well, 2011 yeah, you probably 41.

1782
01:08:28.801 --> 01:08:29.718
42, yeah.

1783
01:08:30.844 --> 01:08:32.719
42 and so then I just wanted to...

1784
01:08:32.719 --> 01:08:34.281
I just needed a mental break whatever

1785
01:08:34.281 --> 01:08:36.851
and so then I ended up writing the book

1786
01:08:36.851 --> 01:08:39.500
and started speaking but I didn't like not do anything,

1787
01:08:39.500 --> 01:08:41.760
I started doing duathlons and marathons.

1788
01:08:41.760 --> 01:08:45.954
'Cause I'm always gonna keep active and if it's important.

1789
01:08:45.954 --> 01:08:48.523
You're making a living in real estate,

1790
01:08:48.523 --> 01:08:49.824
like how do you pay the bills?

1791
01:08:49.824 --> 01:08:51.355
I bought, well, that's what I'm saying,

1792
01:08:51.355 --> 01:08:53.619
that I bought properties young when I was young

1793
01:08:53.619 --> 01:08:55.948
and I was able to own lots of properties.

1794
01:08:55.948 --> 01:08:58.377
That's nice, it's good for the training lifestyle.

1795
01:08:58.377 --> 01:08:59.210
Oh, for sure.

1796
01:08:59.210 --> 01:09:00.924
To have that freedom to do whatever

1797
01:09:00.924 --> 01:09:03.192
you want was important to me.

1798
01:09:03.192 --> 01:09:04.505
But I started doing...

1799
01:09:04.505 --> 01:09:06.211
I was with the Acadian speaking bureau

1800
01:09:06.211 --> 01:09:08.573
so I started doing presentations.

1801
01:09:08.573 --> 01:09:10.938
And just doing other things but then I...

1802
01:09:10.938 --> 01:09:13.500
The itch to come back to RAAM and to write it better

1803
01:09:13.500 --> 01:09:15.448
because I think the first time you do it,

1804
01:09:15.448 --> 01:09:17.274
it's a learning experience.

1805
01:09:17.274 --> 01:09:20.659
And I was just determined to go after the 10 day record.

1806
01:09:20.659 --> 01:09:22.318
It was just something that,

1807
01:09:22.318 --> 01:09:24.432
but I think I just took a little bit too much time off.

1808
01:09:24.432 --> 01:09:25.942
I took almost seven years off the bike

1809
01:09:25.942 --> 01:09:28.940
and then I went back into the ultra endurance racing.

1810
01:09:28.940 --> 01:09:32.190
Right, so the next RAAM was 2019, 18?

1811
01:09:33.355 --> 01:09:34.188
Correct, yeah.

1812
01:09:34.188 --> 01:09:35.256
It was on my 50th birthday,

1813
01:09:35.256 --> 01:09:37.165
I said, I'm gonna gift myself a present.

1814
01:09:37.165 --> 01:09:41.819
50th birthday and you had this experience under your belt.

1815
01:09:41.819 --> 01:09:43.558
Like so how does that change

1816
01:09:43.558 --> 01:09:46.500
how you prepare for the second attempt?

1817
01:09:46.500 --> 01:09:50.267
It was hard because a lot of time had gone by.

1818
01:09:50.267 --> 01:09:51.100
You know what I mean?

1819
01:09:51.100 --> 01:09:52.950
And I think to be out of the loop for that long.

1820
01:09:52.950 --> 01:09:54.224
But I wanted to do...

1821
01:09:54.224 --> 01:09:57.609
And there's a big difference between 50 and 42 and 41.

1822
01:09:57.609 --> 01:09:59.836
Yeah, totally agree, right.

1823
01:09:59.836 --> 01:10:01.609
So what I wanna do is prove to myself

1824
01:10:01.609 --> 01:10:02.816
that I could still ride my bike.

1825
01:10:02.816 --> 01:10:06.606
So I went into Silver State 508, the one in Nevada.

1826
01:10:06.606 --> 01:10:09.212
And I kinda crammed in training and I ended up setting

1827
01:10:09.212 --> 01:10:10.454
a new woman's record and I said,

1828
01:10:10.454 --> 01:10:12.729
okay, I can still ride my bike.

1829
01:10:12.729 --> 01:10:16.458
But I think the prep going into the 2019 RAAM,

1830
01:10:16.458 --> 01:10:20.385
a little bit over-trained, overdid it, I was too excited

1831
01:10:20.385 --> 01:10:23.958
and yeah, so I think we came in there...

1832
01:10:23.958 --> 01:10:26.844
I came in there a little bit cupped.

1833
01:10:26.844 --> 01:10:30.106
But you broke the women's record by like 12 hours.

1834
01:10:30.106 --> 01:10:31.847
No, no, no.
Oh, you didn't?

1835
01:10:31.847 --> 01:10:34.063
Not in that race.
Not in that race.

1836
01:10:34.063 --> 01:10:36.449
I read that wrong then.

1837
01:10:36.449 --> 01:10:41.116
No, that was RAW, that was RAW, the first RAW in 2012.

1838
01:10:42.459 --> 01:10:45.070
But in the 2019 race though,

1839
01:10:45.070 --> 01:10:47.919
it was the hardest women's field the race had ever had.

1840
01:10:47.919 --> 01:10:51.368
There was I think six RAAM champions in there.

1841
01:10:51.368 --> 01:10:52.785
So it was a race.

1842
01:10:53.666 --> 01:10:56.025
Like right from the beginning right to the end.

1843
01:10:56.025 --> 01:10:58.021
And so Isah and Alexandra

1844
01:10:58.021 --> 01:10:59.564
who's already the younger group category

1845
01:10:59.564 --> 01:11:04.039
from Sweden and Austria were the favorites to win

1846
01:11:04.039 --> 01:11:06.353
but it ended up to be Daniella and I,

1847
01:11:06.353 --> 01:11:09.410
with them like 24 hours back.

1848
01:11:09.410 --> 01:11:11.844
But a lot of things happen and so again,

1849
01:11:11.844 --> 01:11:14.226
I think we had issues navigational stuff

1850
01:11:14.226 --> 01:11:16.263
and I ended up losing by 90 minutes

1851
01:11:16.263 --> 01:11:19.664
and that's why I had decided that I'm coming back again.

1852
01:11:19.664 --> 01:11:21.136
90 minutes.
90 minutes.

1853
01:11:21.136 --> 01:11:22.513
After all of that.

1854
01:11:22.513 --> 01:11:25.775
And what you are 11 plus days or something like that?

1855
01:11:25.775 --> 01:11:27.047
No, 10 Days.
10 days.

1856
01:11:27.047 --> 01:11:29.177
10 days yeah, 19 hours.

1857
01:11:29.177 --> 01:11:31.763
And the record is like seven plus-

1858
01:11:31.763 --> 01:11:32.853
No, that's for the men.

1859
01:11:32.853 --> 01:11:36.978
I know for the men, like the fastest time ever recorded.

1860
01:11:36.978 --> 01:11:40.697
The fastest time on that course, not on the course

1861
01:11:40.697 --> 01:11:42.670
that was shorter, on that course is 10 is days.

1862
01:11:42.670 --> 01:11:43.961
Oh it is.
Yeah it is 10 days.

1863
01:11:43.961 --> 01:11:46.091
For men and women.
No for women.

1864
01:11:46.091 --> 01:11:48.051
But for men, like who's the fastest has ever gone?

1865
01:11:48.051 --> 01:11:50.206
I believe Strasser of seven days.

1866
01:11:50.206 --> 01:11:52.104
Yeah, he has for that, yeah.

1867
01:11:52.104 --> 01:11:53.704
So there's two different courses,

1868
01:11:53.704 --> 01:11:55.905
there is a shorter course of Shannon Hogan holds

1869
01:11:55.905 --> 01:11:59.171
that record, but they go based on average speed.

1870
01:11:59.171 --> 01:12:00.641
But we're talking about just the course,

1871
01:12:00.641 --> 01:12:03.482
but for that course, it's Nicole Reese from Sweden,

1872
01:12:03.482 --> 01:12:07.792
sorry, from Austria, she has the course record of 10 days.

1873
01:12:07.792 --> 01:12:11.625
To make it across on that course in 11 days,

1874
01:12:14.548 --> 01:12:16.424
just for people that are listening,

1875
01:12:16.424 --> 01:12:18.600
like you have to be averaging at least

1876
01:12:18.600 --> 01:12:20.630
like 12 miles an hour including sleep

1877
01:12:20.630 --> 01:12:22.384
for the whole thing, right?

1878
01:12:22.384 --> 01:12:24.177
Pretty close, yeah.
Yeah.

1879
01:12:24.177 --> 01:12:26.523
So that provides a little context.

1880
01:12:26.523 --> 01:12:28.187
Right, right, oh yeah.

1881
01:12:28.187 --> 01:12:29.293
Wow.

1882
01:12:29.293 --> 01:12:32.126
So 2019, you re-establish yourself

1883
01:12:34.455 --> 01:12:37.651
but also like a sense of unfinished business?

1884
01:12:37.651 --> 01:12:38.930
Absolutely.
Yeah.

1885
01:12:38.930 --> 01:12:39.763
Yeah, I was determined-

1886
01:12:39.763 --> 01:12:40.596
This cannot stand.

1887
01:12:40.596 --> 01:12:43.026
Well, I mean like you said...

1888
01:12:43.026 --> 01:12:44.611
I just had to come back, right?

1889
01:12:44.611 --> 01:12:46.996
I mean, I wrote down everything that,

1890
01:12:46.996 --> 01:12:49.131
the mistakes that we made, the mistakes that I made.

1891
01:12:49.131 --> 01:12:50.402
What mistakes did you make?

1892
01:12:50.402 --> 01:12:51.879
Well for me I was overconfident.

1893
01:12:51.879 --> 01:12:54.188
I mean, I think I focused way too much on climbing.

1894
01:12:54.188 --> 01:12:55.632
I mean RAAM has got a lot of climbing

1895
01:12:55.632 --> 01:12:56.904
but there's a lot of flats going through Kansas

1896
01:12:56.904 --> 01:12:58.876
and Illinois and whatnot.

1897
01:12:58.876 --> 01:13:00.408
To work more on power,

1898
01:13:00.408 --> 01:13:02.019
we had to get a better navigational system

1899
01:13:02.019 --> 01:13:04.104
'cause I think we probably had about two and a half

1900
01:13:04.104 --> 01:13:06.634
to almost three hours of navigational error,

1901
01:13:06.634 --> 01:13:08.857
which hurt us quite badly.

1902
01:13:08.857 --> 01:13:10.717
Like going off course, you mean?.

1903
01:13:10.717 --> 01:13:11.739
Correct, yeah.

1904
01:13:11.739 --> 01:13:12.769
And when you go off course,

1905
01:13:12.769 --> 01:13:14.566
you have to jump back into cargo back to a place

1906
01:13:14.566 --> 01:13:16.946
where you made a mistake and then start from there, right?

1907
01:13:16.946 --> 01:13:20.213
So on day six we messed up four times.

1908
01:13:20.213 --> 01:13:22.021
And so, I mean-

1909
01:13:22.021 --> 01:13:23.243
And you have the same crew though.

1910
01:13:23.243 --> 01:13:25.125
You have the same crew.

1911
01:13:25.125 --> 01:13:28.948
I mean the same crew that you had in 2011.

1912
01:13:28.948 --> 01:13:32.972
A little bit changes here and there, like a few people.

1913
01:13:32.972 --> 01:13:36.711
But when there was so many detours too because of the floods

1914
01:13:36.711 --> 01:13:38.708
in the Mississippi, you know what I mean?

1915
01:13:38.708 --> 01:13:40.394
And that we got notification a little bit later,

1916
01:13:40.394 --> 01:13:41.227
but you know what?

1917
01:13:41.227 --> 01:13:42.782
That's the nature of the race, it's RAAM,

1918
01:13:42.782 --> 01:13:44.605
it's 3000 miles, you're gonna mess up.

1919
01:13:44.605 --> 01:13:46.077
There's always gonna be some crazy stuff.

1920
01:13:46.077 --> 01:13:48.052
You just wanna catch it really fast, right?

1921
01:13:48.052 --> 01:13:50.877
So we worked on the navigational issue part for this,

1922
01:13:50.877 --> 01:13:53.877
like for the Ram 2021 or 2022 sorry.

1923
01:13:55.271 --> 01:13:58.897
Yeah and then again, I came in way better but don't forget,

1924
01:13:58.897 --> 01:14:01.938
we had the COVID year where RAAM...

1925
01:14:01.938 --> 01:14:04.289
We were all set to go and then we got notification in April

1926
01:14:04.289 --> 01:14:05.640
that the race was canceled.

1927
01:14:05.640 --> 01:14:07.987
But I continued to train like I was racing

1928
01:14:07.987 --> 01:14:11.032
'cause I knew the benefits would lead me into to this year.

1929
01:14:11.032 --> 01:14:13.282
To use that as a benefit.

1930
01:14:14.506 --> 01:14:15.430
Sorry.

1931
01:14:15.430 --> 01:14:17.162
The idea that the race got canceled rather

1932
01:14:17.162 --> 01:14:19.113
than looking at that as a setback,

1933
01:14:19.113 --> 01:14:21.055
like, oh I have a whole nother year to train.

1934
01:14:21.055 --> 01:14:22.743
Absolutely, yeah.

1935
01:14:22.743 --> 01:14:24.614
So I even came back even I could even do more

1936
01:14:24.614 --> 01:14:28.562
to up my training even more in the next following year.

1937
01:14:28.562 --> 01:14:29.603
Yeah.

1938
01:14:29.603 --> 01:14:32.353
Sustaining power on the flats is,

1939
01:14:34.626 --> 01:14:37.212
like seems to be a key to success

1940
01:14:37.212 --> 01:14:39.373
because there are so many flats.

1941
01:14:39.373 --> 01:14:42.052
I think in cycling, you know climbing,

1942
01:14:42.052 --> 01:14:44.119
it's a lot of attention, people can climb well.

1943
01:14:44.119 --> 01:14:46.870
But it's actually really hard to sustain

1944
01:14:46.870 --> 01:14:49.613
like threshold power on flats for a long period of time.

1945
01:14:49.613 --> 01:14:51.708
Like the level of pain that goes into that-

1946
01:14:51.708 --> 01:14:52.643
Oh my God, absolutely.

1947
01:14:52.643 --> 01:14:57.352
Exceeds often like what it's required to attack a climb.

1948
01:14:57.352 --> 01:14:58.401
Yeah, absolutely.

1949
01:14:58.401 --> 01:14:59.234
Oh for sure.

1950
01:14:59.234 --> 01:15:01.984
I mean, but even with climbing...

1951
01:15:03.186 --> 01:15:04.555
Like my crew chief always says,

1952
01:15:04.555 --> 01:15:06.255
the only time you're gonna feel good in RAAM

1953
01:15:06.255 --> 01:15:07.816
is the first few days.

1954
01:15:07.816 --> 01:15:12.030
After that, you're running off fumes it's mental.

1955
01:15:12.030 --> 01:15:14.464
So you're not climbing like you're gonna climb on a timer

1956
01:15:14.464 --> 01:15:17.435
or whatever but to hold that sustained power,

1957
01:15:17.435 --> 01:15:20.663
like you said on the flats especially when you're in Kansas

1958
01:15:20.663 --> 01:15:22.837
and you have a headwind for 19 hours,

1959
01:15:22.837 --> 01:15:25.436
that's more difficult than climbing Wolf Creek Pass

1960
01:15:25.436 --> 01:15:27.708
or whatever in Colorado that's 10,000 feet.

1961
01:15:27.708 --> 01:15:30.179
So, you really have to balance what you're gonna do

1962
01:15:30.179 --> 01:15:32.255
and you can't neglect any element

1963
01:15:32.255 --> 01:15:37.214
where I was only focusing on climbing in the 2011 or 2019.

1964
01:15:37.214 --> 01:15:39.549
But this time I really balanced it out between the two

1965
01:15:39.549 --> 01:15:43.242
so it was much more powerful but also bike positioning.

1966
01:15:43.242 --> 01:15:45.817
You know, being aero is one thing, it's great.

1967
01:15:45.817 --> 01:15:47.555
But if you're uncomfortable after like staying-

1968
01:15:47.555 --> 01:15:49.067
No, comfort is way more important.

1969
01:15:49.067 --> 01:15:49.900
Oh absolutely.

1970
01:15:49.900 --> 01:15:52.067
So I worked on a better...

1971
01:15:52.913 --> 01:15:57.629
Rob Bright, he set me up how a RAAM rider should feel.

1972
01:15:57.629 --> 01:15:59.644
Which is more upright.
More upright.

1973
01:15:59.644 --> 01:16:00.983
Less pressure on your wrists.

1974
01:16:00.983 --> 01:16:03.816
Absolutely.
And your shoulders.

1975
01:16:04.869 --> 01:16:07.174
Yeah, that has to be key getting that right

1976
01:16:07.174 --> 01:16:08.725
and also finding the right saddle

1977
01:16:08.725 --> 01:16:10.532
'cause you're gonna get saddle sores no matter what.

1978
01:16:10.532 --> 01:16:12.543
Oh, you bet, there's no way around it.

1979
01:16:12.543 --> 01:16:14.728
How many saddles did you test before you found one

1980
01:16:14.728 --> 01:16:15.592
that was suitable?

1981
01:16:15.592 --> 01:16:17.373
I just used my one particular saddle

1982
01:16:17.373 --> 01:16:18.648
that just works for me.

1983
01:16:18.648 --> 01:16:21.024
It doesn't matter what saddle, it's just comfortable.

1984
01:16:21.024 --> 01:16:23.335
But you know, a saddle sore is a pressure point

1985
01:16:23.335 --> 01:16:24.677
and you're sitting on that pressure point

1986
01:16:24.677 --> 01:16:26.537
for so long that you're gonna get it no matter what,

1987
01:16:26.537 --> 01:16:29.847
but I think trying to prevent it from progressing too much

1988
01:16:29.847 --> 01:16:32.087
so sitting in Epsom salts really fast,

1989
01:16:32.087 --> 01:16:34.090
changing shorts as much as possible.

1990
01:16:34.090 --> 01:16:35.257
And then in worst case scenario,

1991
01:16:35.257 --> 01:16:37.261
you know you have the Lidocaine, right?

1992
01:16:37.261 --> 01:16:39.616
You just numb it all up.
Numb it up, yeah.

1993
01:16:39.616 --> 01:16:41.644
Oh God, it's the worst.

1994
01:16:41.644 --> 01:16:43.450
It is but you have to do, you have to do.

1995
01:16:43.450 --> 01:16:44.283
Yeah.

1996
01:16:44.283 --> 01:16:46.910
And it's that thing where it's the tiny things

1997
01:16:46.910 --> 01:16:48.717
that fell the journey, right?

1998
01:16:48.717 --> 01:16:49.976
It's the little tiny...

1999
01:16:49.976 --> 01:16:52.966
Oh, I have this little boil but then it's like,

2000
01:16:52.966 --> 01:16:54.750
it becomes your whole universe.

2001
01:16:54.750 --> 01:16:58.007
It's on your mind.
It capsizes the whole thing.

2002
01:16:58.007 --> 01:17:00.150
Totally, the tiniest little thing.

2003
01:17:00.150 --> 01:17:01.138
Yeah.
Yeah.

2004
01:17:01.138 --> 01:17:02.100
Wow.

2005
01:17:02.100 --> 01:17:05.251
What is the, sleep deprivation aspect aside

2006
01:17:05.251 --> 01:17:08.668
like how do you prepare mentally and like

2007
01:17:10.035 --> 01:17:14.191
what are the mantras that you repeat or what is going on

2008
01:17:14.191 --> 01:17:18.263
in your mind when you're living in that pain

2009
01:17:18.263 --> 01:17:20.086
for such an extended period of time

2010
01:17:20.086 --> 01:17:23.372
like are you telling yourself stories?

2011
01:17:23.372 --> 01:17:25.478
Are you just focused on your heart rate?

2012
01:17:25.478 --> 01:17:27.259
Like what is going on in your head?

2013
01:17:27.259 --> 01:17:28.802
I'm focused on a number of things.

2014
01:17:28.802 --> 01:17:31.649
Speed is a big thing but again it all depends on wind

2015
01:17:31.649 --> 01:17:32.649
and terrain.

2016
01:17:34.041 --> 01:17:35.447
If you're descending, if you're climbing.

2017
01:17:35.447 --> 01:17:37.541
I think too, somebody asked me that question before too

2018
01:17:37.541 --> 01:17:40.129
is like when you are that uncomfortable.

2019
01:17:40.129 --> 01:17:41.144
But I think, my mantras,

2020
01:17:41.144 --> 01:17:43.299
you have to learn to be comfortable being uncomfortable,

2021
01:17:43.299 --> 01:17:44.902
that's RAAM, right you know.

2022
01:17:44.902 --> 01:17:46.944
And a lot of times when we are in pain,

2023
01:17:46.944 --> 01:17:49.472
we focus on that pain, that's all we're thinking about.

2024
01:17:49.472 --> 01:17:52.917
So I try to kinda park it and not focus on that pain.

2025
01:17:52.917 --> 01:17:56.381
I know it sounds kinda strange but it works for me.

2026
01:17:56.381 --> 01:17:59.310
And thinking about the task in hand like what am I...

2027
01:17:59.310 --> 01:18:01.104
What have I done to get here?

2028
01:18:01.104 --> 01:18:02.146
The training that I've gone through

2029
01:18:02.146 --> 01:18:04.186
and you wanna get to that finish line, right?

2030
01:18:04.186 --> 01:18:05.968
So stop dwelling on the things that hurt you

2031
01:18:05.968 --> 01:18:07.530
and let's focused on what you need to do

2032
01:18:07.530 --> 01:18:08.972
to get to your finish line.

2033
01:18:08.972 --> 01:18:11.846
And I think that really worked for me.

2034
01:18:11.846 --> 01:18:15.721
And just yeah, I think preparing in the right way,

2035
01:18:15.721 --> 01:18:18.981
knowing what I was doing prior to is a big part

2036
01:18:18.981 --> 01:18:21.981
of completing RAAM in 10 to 12 days.

2037
01:18:23.154 --> 01:18:23.987
Yeah.

2038
01:18:23.987 --> 01:18:25.758
You said you've never quit anything

2039
01:18:25.758 --> 01:18:28.467
but I suspect there have been moments of weakness

2040
01:18:28.467 --> 01:18:30.365
where you were on the precipice of quitting

2041
01:18:30.365 --> 01:18:32.637
like how do you talk yourself off the ledge

2042
01:18:32.637 --> 01:18:34.599
in those situations?
Easy.

2043
01:18:34.599 --> 01:18:36.825
Or you just, you don't even get there.

2044
01:18:36.825 --> 01:18:39.609
No, of course, I mean...

2045
01:18:39.609 --> 01:18:42.069
The only thing I have to think about is how I'll regret it

2046
01:18:42.069 --> 01:18:43.994
and I'll just dwell on it for the rest of my life.

2047
01:18:43.994 --> 01:18:46.140
But it's like you've held that line,

2048
01:18:46.140 --> 01:18:49.348
like I've never quit anything so I'm not gonna start now.

2049
01:18:49.348 --> 01:18:50.600
Patterns are repeated.

2050
01:18:50.600 --> 01:18:53.580
There's no fissures in that wall.

2051
01:18:53.580 --> 01:18:56.370
Yeah, to me quitting should never be an option

2052
01:18:56.370 --> 01:18:58.169
unless it's life-threatening of course,

2053
01:18:58.169 --> 01:19:00.549
that's my mantra I've.

2054
01:19:00.549 --> 01:19:01.756
But what if you're...

2055
01:19:01.756 --> 01:19:04.266
I mean, there are situations in your life I'm sure

2056
01:19:04.266 --> 01:19:09.243
where you were pursuing something and you realize like,

2057
01:19:09.243 --> 01:19:11.946
well, this is not for me or this is not right.

2058
01:19:11.946 --> 01:19:13.703
Like it's not really quitting,

2059
01:19:13.703 --> 01:19:15.370
it's sort of changing directions.

2060
01:19:15.370 --> 01:19:18.763
I think people sometimes stay in things too long,

2061
01:19:18.763 --> 01:19:20.362
they probably should quit stuff sooner

2062
01:19:20.362 --> 01:19:21.464
and try something else.

2063
01:19:21.464 --> 01:19:22.362
Yeah, that's different.

2064
01:19:22.362 --> 01:19:23.663
So how do you differentiate?

2065
01:19:23.663 --> 01:19:26.026
I mean, I think whatever you choose to do,

2066
01:19:26.026 --> 01:19:27.427
you have to love it.

2067
01:19:27.427 --> 01:19:30.648
I think people like you were saying who go to a job,

2068
01:19:30.648 --> 01:19:31.481
they wake up in the morning

2069
01:19:31.481 --> 01:19:33.112
and they hate what they're doing, right?

2070
01:19:33.112 --> 01:19:35.983
I mean I find that sad and I think life is too short.

2071
01:19:35.983 --> 01:19:37.182
I mean, that happened to me in Israel.

2072
01:19:37.182 --> 01:19:39.350
My dream was to be a Belush agent

2073
01:19:39.350 --> 01:19:41.687
and work for intelligence till I was gonna retire,

2074
01:19:41.687 --> 01:19:46.687
45, you have a great pension in Israel, that was my dream.

2075
01:19:46.749 --> 01:19:49.440
I think having a purpose to what I was doing and loving

2076
01:19:49.440 --> 01:19:51.273
what I was doing was more important for me

2077
01:19:51.273 --> 01:19:53.222
than the ego of that job.

2078
01:19:53.222 --> 01:19:54.318
And I think whatever you choose,

2079
01:19:54.318 --> 01:19:55.755
do choose to do that you love it,

2080
01:19:55.755 --> 01:19:57.127
that when you wake up in the morning,

2081
01:19:57.127 --> 01:19:59.571
you have a motivation and a desire 'cause life's too short.

2082
01:19:59.571 --> 01:20:01.432
I mean, this is a one-time deal, this is our life,

2083
01:20:01.432 --> 01:20:02.529
let's make the best of it.

2084
01:20:02.529 --> 01:20:05.698
And so that kind of is my mantra forever.

2085
01:20:05.698 --> 01:20:08.271
That's how I live my life.

2086
01:20:08.271 --> 01:20:11.771
But when you're on hour 45 without sleep

2087
01:20:12.934 --> 01:20:15.841
and you forget where you are and you're not sure

2088
01:20:15.841 --> 01:20:17.726
what you're doing or what your feet are doing,

2089
01:20:17.726 --> 01:20:20.277
like how do you maintain that focus?

2090
01:20:20.277 --> 01:20:22.886
Like are there tactics or strategies that you deploy

2091
01:20:22.886 --> 01:20:26.008
to bring yourself back into your body

2092
01:20:26.008 --> 01:20:29.012
and remind yourself why you're doing it?

2093
01:20:29.012 --> 01:20:30.452
Like, what does that look like?

2094
01:20:30.452 --> 01:20:32.151
Like when we're talking about RAAM,

2095
01:20:32.151 --> 01:20:34.045
sometimes at that point, it's up to the crew

2096
01:20:34.045 --> 01:20:37.219
to say, you know what, you need 30 minutes off the bike.

2097
01:20:37.219 --> 01:20:39.249
When you're not moving and you're delirious

2098
01:20:39.249 --> 01:20:40.966
and it can be dangerous where like I mean,

2099
01:20:40.966 --> 01:20:42.460
at one point I was hallucinating so bad

2100
01:20:42.460 --> 01:20:43.956
that a Panther had jumped in front of me

2101
01:20:43.956 --> 01:20:46.202
that I veered over to the other side of the road.

2102
01:20:46.202 --> 01:20:48.414
So in that case, my crew is gonna stop me,

2103
01:20:48.414 --> 01:20:49.457
that's why it's very important,

2104
01:20:49.457 --> 01:20:52.287
there behind me all the time and I'm radioed to them.

2105
01:20:52.287 --> 01:20:54.181
And they'll say, you need 30 minutes.

2106
01:20:54.181 --> 01:20:56.260
So your crew is your lifeline, they're your brain

2107
01:20:56.260 --> 01:20:58.077
'cause you become like a puppet.

2108
01:20:58.077 --> 01:21:01.164
So that's why you have good communication before the race

2109
01:21:01.164 --> 01:21:03.392
of what's gonna transpire

2110
01:21:03.392 --> 01:21:07.642
if you can no longer make those rational decisions.

2111
01:21:08.522 --> 01:21:10.654
But, we all know what I'm there for.

2112
01:21:10.654 --> 01:21:12.955
And of course if it was life-threatening or dangerous,

2113
01:21:12.955 --> 01:21:15.940
my crew would pull me but it would...

2114
01:21:15.940 --> 01:21:18.028
That's the only way that they would ever pull me.

2115
01:21:18.028 --> 01:21:20.141
(indistinct)

2116
01:21:20.141 --> 01:21:21.055
If it wasn't safe.

2117
01:21:21.055 --> 01:21:21.888
Right.

2118
01:21:21.888 --> 01:21:24.221
On the Shermer's neck thing,

2119
01:21:25.358 --> 01:21:28.268
you trained for the most recent RAAM

2120
01:21:28.268 --> 01:21:30.550
by wearing a weighted helmet, right?

2121
01:21:30.550 --> 01:21:31.383
Correct, yes.

2122
01:21:31.383 --> 01:21:33.200
To try to strengthen those neck muscles.

2123
01:21:33.200 --> 01:21:36.425
I did intense strength exercises with my...

2124
01:21:36.425 --> 01:21:39.256
Not only that, it's like those...

2125
01:21:39.256 --> 01:21:40.821
It's like a harness you wear over your head

2126
01:21:40.821 --> 01:21:43.238
and it's got weighted plates.

2127
01:21:44.813 --> 01:21:47.997
I do a lot of like reps out like that (indistinct).

2128
01:21:47.997 --> 01:21:50.178
And always, always when I'm on the trainer,

2129
01:21:50.178 --> 01:21:52.121
I always have a one pound helmet on.

2130
01:21:52.121 --> 01:21:54.306
You'll see I have three little plates on top of my helmet.

2131
01:21:54.306 --> 01:21:56.204
And when I ride, I have one little plate,

2132
01:21:56.204 --> 01:21:57.905
so I have different weights.

2133
01:21:57.905 --> 01:22:01.143
And so I always have some kind of load on my head.

2134
01:22:01.143 --> 01:22:03.147
So when I wear the helmet, it feels like nothing.

2135
01:22:03.147 --> 01:22:04.414
And that does help.

2136
01:22:04.414 --> 01:22:07.585
It's not a complete cure, but it does make your neck

2137
01:22:07.585 --> 01:22:09.228
a little bit stronger for sure.

2138
01:22:09.228 --> 01:22:10.387
Still gotta shave the head and-

2139
01:22:10.387 --> 01:22:12.387
Still gotta shave the head, yeah.

2140
01:22:12.387 --> 01:22:15.804
All right, so going into the 2021 RAAM,

2141
01:22:17.849 --> 01:22:19.349
what was the goal?

2142
01:22:20.804 --> 01:22:22.721
The goal was 10 days,

2143
01:22:23.629 --> 01:22:26.712
you know that was kinda the big goal.

2144
01:22:27.730 --> 01:22:32.072
I think that in to get the women's 50 record right.

2145
01:22:32.072 --> 01:22:34.604
And make it across within the 12 days

2146
01:22:34.604 --> 01:22:37.203
of course and be safe.

2147
01:22:37.203 --> 01:22:39.674
It's really hard to plan 'cause you don't know

2148
01:22:39.674 --> 01:22:41.591
what you're gonna face.

2149
01:22:43.442 --> 01:22:47.166
We went 10 days earlier for the heat to kinda climatize

2150
01:22:47.166 --> 01:22:48.420
to those temperatures to break us.

2151
01:22:48.420 --> 01:22:49.658
You knew it was gonna be a hot year.

2152
01:22:49.658 --> 01:22:52.015
Well, I mean there was a heat wave that was...

2153
01:22:52.015 --> 01:22:54.001
We didn't know like it was gonna be that hot,

2154
01:22:54.001 --> 01:22:55.604
I don't think anybody knew, right?

2155
01:22:55.604 --> 01:22:59.666
A week before, it was like 90 degrees in Borrego Springs

2156
01:22:59.666 --> 01:23:01.012
which was quite pleasant.

2157
01:23:01.012 --> 01:23:03.382
I have that at home, do you know what I mean?

2158
01:23:03.382 --> 01:23:05.508
But then all of a sudden, we checked the forecast

2159
01:23:05.508 --> 01:23:08.970
and the day of the race, like when RAAM started,

2160
01:23:08.970 --> 01:23:11.971
it was about 85 and then as soon as we hit the desert,

2161
01:23:11.971 --> 01:23:15.965
like four hours later, it went from 85 to 110.

2162
01:23:15.965 --> 01:23:18.171
I mean, descending down the glass elevator,

2163
01:23:18.171 --> 01:23:20.491
I could feel my eyeballs burning.

2164
01:23:20.491 --> 01:23:22.748
And I don't think anybody was prepared

2165
01:23:22.748 --> 01:23:25.540
for what we were getting into this year.

2166
01:23:25.540 --> 01:23:26.373
Yeah.

2167
01:23:26.373 --> 01:23:29.470
And if it's 110, then the pavement's probably 120.

2168
01:23:29.470 --> 01:23:30.547
Oh my gosh, yeah.

2169
01:23:30.547 --> 01:23:33.085
And just the reflection off the pavement,

2170
01:23:33.085 --> 01:23:34.621
the heat is super oppressive.

2171
01:23:34.621 --> 01:23:36.806
It was the most intense feeling I'd ever...

2172
01:23:36.806 --> 01:23:39.309
I mean, even in Israel where it's very difficult and hot,

2173
01:23:39.309 --> 01:23:42.034
we'd never experienced anything like that before.

2174
01:23:42.034 --> 01:23:45.260
And the trick, the thing was the heat didn't abate,

2175
01:23:45.260 --> 01:23:47.207
like it wasn't just through the desert,

2176
01:23:47.207 --> 01:23:50.012
like it was an unprecedented heat wave

2177
01:23:50.012 --> 01:23:52.177
for the entire experiment.

2178
01:23:52.177 --> 01:23:54.447
Even at nighttime, I mean it...

2179
01:23:54.447 --> 01:23:56.632
I mean the only pleasantry at night

2180
01:23:56.632 --> 01:23:58.621
is that you didn't have the blazing sun on you

2181
01:23:58.621 --> 01:24:01.949
but it was still excruciating hot,

2182
01:24:01.949 --> 01:24:03.847
like it was just...

2183
01:24:03.847 --> 01:24:06.379
I mean in Kansas I burned right through my jersey,

2184
01:24:06.379 --> 01:24:08.923
it was that intense, the heat was that intense.

2185
01:24:08.923 --> 01:24:11.530
So with that, the challenge is

2186
01:24:11.530 --> 01:24:15.895
how do I keep my core temperature from overheating?

2187
01:24:15.895 --> 01:24:19.808
So what strategies did you deploy with ice and the like

2188
01:24:19.808 --> 01:24:23.645
to try to ameliorate your body just boiling over?

2189
01:24:23.645 --> 01:24:25.204
Well, for one, the jersey.

2190
01:24:25.204 --> 01:24:27.567
Like jerseys aren't very friendly for hot temperature

2191
01:24:27.567 --> 01:24:30.115
so we just use a cotton men's t-shirt.

2192
01:24:30.115 --> 01:24:32.900
You know we had cut slice just to keep it cool.

2193
01:24:32.900 --> 01:24:36.868
I had like a veil, so it wouldn't burn my neck.

2194
01:24:36.868 --> 01:24:38.635
And then in the real intense heat,

2195
01:24:38.635 --> 01:24:42.206
it's like an ice sock, like a long sock like a scarf full

2196
01:24:42.206 --> 01:24:45.062
of ice wrapped around my neck.

2197
01:24:45.062 --> 01:24:48.340
And then every, I'm gonna say three to four miles,

2198
01:24:48.340 --> 01:24:50.058
one of my crew members would stand on the side of a road

2199
01:24:50.058 --> 01:24:52.593
with a bottle of water that I could dump over,

2200
01:24:52.593 --> 01:24:54.598
douche was over my head.

2201
01:24:54.598 --> 01:24:58.106
And I would dry within, I'm gonna say six minutes,

2202
01:24:58.106 --> 01:25:00.837
I would be completely dry.

2203
01:25:00.837 --> 01:25:02.434
And then even that wasn't enough

2204
01:25:02.434 --> 01:25:04.671
so we had to stop a couple of time for IVs,

2205
01:25:04.671 --> 01:25:06.622
I needed about three bags of IVs

2206
01:25:06.622 --> 01:25:09.693
'cause it was getting dangerously dehydrated.

2207
01:25:09.693 --> 01:25:11.044
That's permitted under the rules?

2208
01:25:11.044 --> 01:25:11.893
That's permitted, yeah.

2209
01:25:11.893 --> 01:25:13.901
And the RAAM rules are pretty strict

2210
01:25:13.901 --> 01:25:15.688
in terms of what you can and can't do.

2211
01:25:15.688 --> 01:25:18.443
Correct, but when it's life threatening like that,

2212
01:25:18.443 --> 01:25:19.691
I mean, I think all of us did.

2213
01:25:19.691 --> 01:25:21.669
I mean the two guys that were behind me, they didn't have...

2214
01:25:21.669 --> 01:25:26.145
I had a doctrine nurse with me so they could give me the IV,

2215
01:25:26.145 --> 01:25:29.077
but they also got the IV in the hospital

2216
01:25:29.077 --> 01:25:30.985
and everyone else had dropped out, right?

2217
01:25:30.985 --> 01:25:34.835
Even teams had dropped out early into the race

2218
01:25:34.835 --> 01:25:38.748
especially during through California and Arizona.

2219
01:25:38.748 --> 01:25:40.635
So how many towed the line

2220
01:25:40.635 --> 01:25:43.313
and then how many ended up finishing?

2221
01:25:43.313 --> 01:25:46.146
I finished on Saturday, 11 days.

2222
01:25:47.429 --> 01:25:50.563
I believe it was three hours and then 17 hours after me,

2223
01:25:50.563 --> 01:25:53.097
Eric was the first male and then two hours after him,

2224
01:25:53.097 --> 01:25:54.595
the last rider came in.

2225
01:25:54.595 --> 01:25:57.175
Only three people finished.

2226
01:25:57.175 --> 01:25:59.301
Yeah, and I believe that the teams...

2227
01:25:59.301 --> 01:26:00.345
What about relays?

2228
01:26:00.345 --> 01:26:02.278
I'm not exactly sure don't quote me on this

2229
01:26:02.278 --> 01:26:05.027
but I believe half of them did drop out.

2230
01:26:05.027 --> 01:26:06.496
That's when you know it's bad.

2231
01:26:06.496 --> 01:26:07.329
You know it's bad.

2232
01:26:07.329 --> 01:26:08.665
Because if the relays are dropping out

2233
01:26:08.665 --> 01:26:12.137
where they're getting rest, it's gotta be insane.

2234
01:26:12.137 --> 01:26:14.114
Yeah I mean, just try standing

2235
01:26:14.114 --> 01:26:16.484
in those temperatures let alone ride, right?

2236
01:26:16.484 --> 01:26:18.049
Yeah it was crazy.

2237
01:26:18.049 --> 01:26:21.049
And on top of that like in Kansas...

2238
01:26:22.120 --> 01:26:25.007
'Cause at that point it was between myself and Mark

2239
01:26:25.007 --> 01:26:27.191
who was leading the field at that time,

2240
01:26:27.191 --> 01:26:29.052
he was from the United Kingdom.

2241
01:26:29.052 --> 01:26:31.186
We both had a headwind for like I'm gonna say close

2242
01:26:31.186 --> 01:26:33.756
to 20 hours, it was just relentless.

2243
01:26:33.756 --> 01:26:36.030
Wow, at what point...

2244
01:26:36.030 --> 01:26:40.863
So you complete the race in 11 days, three hours.

2245
01:26:40.863 --> 01:26:45.156
You are the first woman to ever win the race outright

2246
01:26:45.156 --> 01:26:48.418
in the 39 year history of this race.

2247
01:26:48.418 --> 01:26:53.034
At what point during the race did you feel like you had

2248
01:26:53.034 --> 01:26:55.356
a shot at winning?

2249
01:26:55.356 --> 01:26:59.041
Well, it was kind of a way in the back of my head

2250
01:26:59.041 --> 01:27:00.121
in the start, right?

2251
01:27:00.121 --> 01:27:02.328
I mean I knew Mark was a nine dayer, right?

2252
01:27:02.328 --> 01:27:05.700
I mean, he's done it I think, I believe nine times,

2253
01:27:05.700 --> 01:27:07.051
the man from the United Kingdom.

2254
01:27:07.051 --> 01:27:09.384
He was the favorite to win,.

2255
01:27:12.035 --> 01:27:15.145
I mean, when I kind of knew it was

2256
01:27:15.145 --> 01:27:18.113
when I passed him in the Appalachians.

2257
01:27:18.113 --> 01:27:20.998
But I didn't do no victory speech or anything like that

2258
01:27:20.998 --> 01:27:23.156
because anything can happen during RAAM,

2259
01:27:23.156 --> 01:27:23.989
you know what I mean?

2260
01:27:23.989 --> 01:27:27.669
You don't do that because I still had a lot of miles to go.

2261
01:27:27.669 --> 01:27:30.785
The Appalachians is what really separates everybody.

2262
01:27:30.785 --> 01:27:32.934
Yeah, I mean that's the hardest.

2263
01:27:32.934 --> 01:27:34.805
Yeah, the climbs are steeper

2264
01:27:34.805 --> 01:27:36.794
like the rock was kinda more gradual,

2265
01:27:36.794 --> 01:27:38.621
these are steep, sharp climbs

2266
01:27:38.621 --> 01:27:42.786
and it's just climb after climb after climb.

2267
01:27:42.786 --> 01:27:45.906
And I was kind of, when him and I were going back and forth,

2268
01:27:45.906 --> 01:27:48.101
I was kinda trying to conserve as much as possible

2269
01:27:48.101 --> 01:27:52.369
because that's kind of where my strength is the climbing.

2270
01:27:52.369 --> 01:27:55.459
But I think he pulled out before we even...

2271
01:27:55.459 --> 01:27:56.937
As we were getting into the Appalachians,

2272
01:27:56.937 --> 01:28:00.149
I think he pulled out of the race.

2273
01:28:00.149 --> 01:28:03.087
But as you said, anything can happen

2274
01:28:03.087 --> 01:28:08.087
and you almost didn't finish within almost seeing distance

2275
01:28:08.420 --> 01:28:09.511
of the finished line.

2276
01:28:09.511 --> 01:28:10.748
Oh my God, yeah.

2277
01:28:10.748 --> 01:28:12.113
(both laughs)

2278
01:28:12.113 --> 01:28:13.869
Yeah, oh my gosh, yeah.

2279
01:28:13.869 --> 01:28:15.740
I'll never forget that till the day I die.

2280
01:28:15.740 --> 01:28:19.602
So, I mean, I think that day I was really overheated,

2281
01:28:19.602 --> 01:28:21.359
electrolyte balance is super important

2282
01:28:21.359 --> 01:28:24.294
to keep everything intact and I was hot and tired

2283
01:28:24.294 --> 01:28:26.803
and I wasn't eating, I wasn't drink really

2284
01:28:26.803 --> 01:28:28.634
as much as I wanted to and just wanting

2285
01:28:28.634 --> 01:28:30.739
to get the thing over with and all of a sudden,

2286
01:28:30.739 --> 01:28:33.289
my heart just elevated like it was beating

2287
01:28:33.289 --> 01:28:36.253
at 200 beats an hour and I got super busy.

2288
01:28:36.253 --> 01:28:37.553
10 beats a minute, yeah.

2289
01:28:37.553 --> 01:28:38.957
Yeah, yeah, 10 minutes, sorry.

2290
01:28:38.957 --> 01:28:42.247
And I basically fell off my bike onto a patchy grass.

2291
01:28:42.247 --> 01:28:44.922
It was on the right side, thank God it was there you know?

2292
01:28:44.922 --> 01:28:46.277
And I just started to tremble

2293
01:28:46.277 --> 01:28:48.722
and I've never experienced that before.

2294
01:28:48.722 --> 01:28:51.271
And so my crew comes out, they're trying to cool me down

2295
01:28:51.271 --> 01:28:53.438
and I said, you know what?

2296
01:28:54.811 --> 01:28:56.654
And the finish is right there

2297
01:28:56.654 --> 01:28:59.885
and I'm terrified 'cause I can't get up, right?

2298
01:28:59.885 --> 01:29:02.018
And so I relaxed a little bit more than I get up

2299
01:29:02.018 --> 01:29:05.408
and I said you know what, I'm allowed to walk with the bike.

2300
01:29:05.408 --> 01:29:07.651
You can't have any help, so no one touched me so my crew-

2301
01:29:07.651 --> 01:29:10.022
And you got like 10 hours on the guy behind you.

2302
01:29:10.022 --> 01:29:11.378
Yeah, no, I had-

2303
01:29:11.378 --> 01:29:12.853
The least right? More than that?

2304
01:29:12.853 --> 01:29:14.386
At that point I had 17 hours, right.

2305
01:29:14.386 --> 01:29:15.951
But still you wanna come to the finish.

2306
01:29:15.951 --> 01:29:17.658
I mean people are waiting for.

2307
01:29:17.658 --> 01:29:19.239
You know it was crazy.

2308
01:29:19.239 --> 01:29:21.438
So I start walking the bike with...

2309
01:29:21.438 --> 01:29:24.336
So my crew member, she gives me her pink running shoes

2310
01:29:24.336 --> 01:29:26.690
so you can see me walking, there's pictures of me walking

2311
01:29:26.690 --> 01:29:29.173
with the bike and then into the actual finish,

2312
01:29:29.173 --> 01:29:30.939
it's a little bit of a descend.

2313
01:29:30.939 --> 01:29:32.700
So I didn't wanna walk, I was kind of embarrassed

2314
01:29:32.700 --> 01:29:34.791
so I said, you know what, I can get and just roll into.

2315
01:29:34.791 --> 01:29:37.617
So you can see in the real finish of Race Across America,

2316
01:29:37.617 --> 01:29:40.612
I'm rolling in and then you can see two crew members running

2317
01:29:40.612 --> 01:29:43.408
beside me to catch me just in case I fall off my bike.

2318
01:29:43.408 --> 01:29:46.161
If you tipped over and you've got running shoes on.

2319
01:29:46.161 --> 01:29:48.140
I have running shoes on, these big oversized,

2320
01:29:48.140 --> 01:29:50.585
pink running shoes and then as soon as I crossed the line,

2321
01:29:50.585 --> 01:29:52.834
you see the ambulance, the fire department come

2322
01:29:52.834 --> 01:29:55.079
and they're all taking my vitals and wanting me

2323
01:29:55.079 --> 01:29:57.730
to go to the hospital and all that kind of stuff, right?

2324
01:29:57.730 --> 01:29:58.822
So, it was-
Wow.

2325
01:29:58.822 --> 01:30:00.312
But that was honestly, it was a scary moment&lt;

2326
01:30:00.312 --> 01:30:02.613
I was in total panic mode.

2327
01:30:02.613 --> 01:30:04.424
Do you have memory of that?

2328
01:30:04.424 --> 01:30:06.296
I mean sometimes when that kinda stuff happens

2329
01:30:06.296 --> 01:30:08.861
like you're in a semi blackout.

2330
01:30:08.861 --> 01:30:10.115
No, I remember very well.

2331
01:30:10.115 --> 01:30:11.329
You do, you do?

2332
01:30:11.329 --> 01:30:13.408
I remember the panic on my face,

2333
01:30:13.408 --> 01:30:14.981
the panic on my crew's face go,

2334
01:30:14.981 --> 01:30:16.109
oh my God, it's like right there.

2335
01:30:16.109 --> 01:30:18.412
I mean, you can smell it, right?

2336
01:30:18.412 --> 01:30:20.495
That close, but I mean...

2337
01:30:21.356 --> 01:30:23.086
Like I said, I mean I had a pretty good cushion,

2338
01:30:23.086 --> 01:30:26.493
a good lead on the guy behind but you still wanna,

2339
01:30:26.493 --> 01:30:28.294
there's a crowd waiting for you and you know-

2340
01:30:28.294 --> 01:30:30.448
Yeah, you wanna look good crossing that line

2341
01:30:30.448 --> 01:30:31.281
and that's you're gonna...

2342
01:30:31.281 --> 01:30:33.480
The picture is gonna get taken right.

2343
01:30:33.480 --> 01:30:34.612
Yeah, exactly.

2344
01:30:34.612 --> 01:30:39.499
So 52 years old, first woman to ever win this race.

2345
01:30:39.499 --> 01:30:43.749
I mean, what does that say about athletic potential

2346
01:30:45.506 --> 01:30:49.521
as we age and also the difference between men and women

2347
01:30:49.521 --> 01:30:53.231
when it comes to endurance and ultra endurance specifically.

2348
01:30:53.231 --> 01:30:57.624
I mean, women tend to just get better and better and better

2349
01:30:57.624 --> 01:31:02.305
and that gap between men and women narrows considerably

2350
01:31:02.305 --> 01:31:05.893
as you age up and gets eclipsed as in your case.

2351
01:31:05.893 --> 01:31:07.350
I think yeah, I think that's...

2352
01:31:07.350 --> 01:31:08.815
I mean like I said, I'm not a scientist,

2353
01:31:08.815 --> 01:31:11.610
I don't know the science behind it but I believe like

2354
01:31:11.610 --> 01:31:13.372
with a race like Race Across America,

2355
01:31:13.372 --> 01:31:17.364
it's 70% mental, 30% physical.

2356
01:31:17.364 --> 01:31:19.647
And in this particular one even if you talk to Eric

2357
01:31:19.647 --> 01:31:21.385
and I am sorry, I don't know the gentleman

2358
01:31:21.385 --> 01:31:25.201
that came in second, but it was a race of attrition,

2359
01:31:25.201 --> 01:31:26.752
of who can take the most suffering

2360
01:31:26.752 --> 01:31:30.389
because you honestly could not ride as hard as you want to.

2361
01:31:30.389 --> 01:31:31.729
It was survival-

2362
01:31:31.729 --> 01:31:32.690
It was survival exactly,

2363
01:31:32.690 --> 01:31:35.606
so it was a different type of race.

2364
01:31:35.606 --> 01:31:38.945
But like you said, I think the gap does get narrower

2365
01:31:38.945 --> 01:31:40.370
'cause a lot of women have won,

2366
01:31:40.370 --> 01:31:42.967
the transcontinental German lady won the race.

2367
01:31:42.967 --> 01:31:45.997
Some ultra running races, a couple of them have won races.

2368
01:31:45.997 --> 01:31:48.857
So I think the gap does close as you get

2369
01:31:48.857 --> 01:31:50.960
into the longer races where it's not so much

2370
01:31:50.960 --> 01:31:54.534
on your strength but on your mental ability

2371
01:31:54.534 --> 01:31:56.039
and how much pain you can take.

2372
01:31:56.039 --> 01:31:57.664
Yeah, women can take more pain-

2373
01:31:57.664 --> 01:31:58.914
I think well maybe yeah.

2374
01:31:58.914 --> 01:32:00.069
I think so.

2375
01:32:00.069 --> 01:32:03.622
I think you're a living example of that.

2376
01:32:03.622 --> 01:32:05.466
Are you gonna do this again?

2377
01:32:05.466 --> 01:32:06.985
Oh, absolutely.

2378
01:32:06.985 --> 01:32:07.857
I didn't, I mean-

2379
01:32:07.857 --> 01:32:09.268
'Cause you didn't meet your time goal.

2380
01:32:09.268 --> 01:32:12.237
I did not meet my goal so I remember crossing the line

2381
01:32:12.237 --> 01:32:14.045
and I go, oh damn it, I gotta do it again.

2382
01:32:14.045 --> 01:32:15.003
I was getting...

2383
01:32:15.003 --> 01:32:17.843
I'm just fixated on 10 days.

2384
01:32:17.843 --> 01:32:19.110
I just you know...

2385
01:32:19.110 --> 01:32:21.549
I know I'm really pushing the envelope,

2386
01:32:21.549 --> 01:32:24.019
I'm not a spring chicken but I still feel

2387
01:32:24.019 --> 01:32:27.015
that I have the ability to ride it in 10 days.

2388
01:32:27.015 --> 01:32:28.785
And what did you learn with this RAAM

2389
01:32:28.785 --> 01:32:31.499
that will be instructive in terms of tweaking your training

2390
01:32:31.499 --> 01:32:33.324
for the next one?

2391
01:32:33.324 --> 01:32:35.663
I have to learn, it's the heat.

2392
01:32:35.663 --> 01:32:39.270
I mean honestly, if you take the heat away,

2393
01:32:39.270 --> 01:32:41.561
I believe I could've made my goal, I really do.

2394
01:32:41.561 --> 01:32:45.457
I felt like I was smarter this time, my crew was smarter,

2395
01:32:45.457 --> 01:32:47.239
we were prepared navigational wise,

2396
01:32:47.239 --> 01:32:49.406
we made very little error.

2397
01:32:50.867 --> 01:32:52.899
Yeah, I think that it's just...

2398
01:32:52.899 --> 01:32:55.704
I think just the weather conditions is what handicapped us.

2399
01:32:55.704 --> 01:32:58.631
Like I said it wasn't just one section of the race,

2400
01:32:58.631 --> 01:32:59.771
it was right across the country.

2401
01:32:59.771 --> 01:33:00.625
Yeah.

2402
01:33:00.625 --> 01:33:04.837
So when you're done, how long do you then sleep for?

2403
01:33:04.837 --> 01:33:06.987
Or is your body just so messed up that you can only sleep

2404
01:33:06.987 --> 01:33:08.716
like an hour and then you're up again?

2405
01:33:08.716 --> 01:33:10.259
Your body is pretty messed up.

2406
01:33:10.259 --> 01:33:13.071
It took me, I'm gonna say almost a week before

2407
01:33:13.071 --> 01:33:15.501
I could go into that regular cycle.

2408
01:33:15.501 --> 01:33:16.409
'Cause you're in this trauma,

2409
01:33:16.409 --> 01:33:19.698
it's like your body's trying to protect you from dying.

2410
01:33:19.698 --> 01:33:22.155
And it takes a while before it realizes like,

2411
01:33:22.155 --> 01:33:25.337
oh, the heat's off, is there a cra...

2412
01:33:25.337 --> 01:33:26.905
And then there's a crash, right?

2413
01:33:26.905 --> 01:33:27.797
Yeah.

2414
01:33:27.797 --> 01:33:29.880
Well, I mean I think too it's important.

2415
01:33:29.880 --> 01:33:32.950
Like, I got on the bike pretty fast

2416
01:33:32.950 --> 01:33:36.275
when I felt like I had some pretty serious saddle sores.

2417
01:33:36.275 --> 01:33:38.046
But it just spinning just really lights being

2418
01:33:38.046 --> 01:33:39.295
is moving the body.

2419
01:33:39.295 --> 01:33:41.060
So I don't think you want to cold Turkey it,

2420
01:33:41.060 --> 01:33:42.268
I don't think it's good for the body.

2421
01:33:42.268 --> 01:33:44.856
You know I think you'll recover faster if you stay...

2422
01:33:44.856 --> 01:33:46.714
'Cause you know, you think about all the training leading up

2423
01:33:46.714 --> 01:33:47.792
to this race, right.

2424
01:33:47.792 --> 01:33:50.273
And just to do nothing, I think that is worse

2425
01:33:50.273 --> 01:33:52.578
for you than actually getting back and doing so I did.

2426
01:33:52.578 --> 01:33:54.711
I mean because of COVID I had to quarantine

2427
01:33:54.711 --> 01:33:56.881
so I did a lot of trainer workouts at home

2428
01:33:56.881 --> 01:33:57.987
for the two weeks.

2429
01:33:57.987 --> 01:33:59.560
But I think that really helped me recover

2430
01:33:59.560 --> 01:34:01.314
and again as I was telling you,

2431
01:34:01.314 --> 01:34:03.722
I think it was the body, I couldn't push myself

2432
01:34:03.722 --> 01:34:04.811
as hard as I wanted to.

2433
01:34:04.811 --> 01:34:06.870
So my muscles weren't particularly sore

2434
01:34:06.870 --> 01:34:10.491
as the damage to my skin, my face and whatnot.

2435
01:34:10.491 --> 01:34:11.830
Oh my God.

2436
01:34:11.830 --> 01:34:14.600
And the other thing is you did this plant-based,

2437
01:34:14.600 --> 01:34:15.692
you're a plant-based athlete.

2438
01:34:15.692 --> 01:34:16.550
Correct?
Yeah.

2439
01:34:16.550 --> 01:34:17.996
How long have you been plant-based?

2440
01:34:17.996 --> 01:34:20.490
Well, it's funny, it's been in phases.

2441
01:34:20.490 --> 01:34:22.685
I mean, I actually stopped eating meat

2442
01:34:22.685 --> 01:34:25.066
when I was a little girl.

2443
01:34:25.066 --> 01:34:27.564
I just loved animals so much and you think about it

2444
01:34:27.564 --> 01:34:29.748
in my generation, at five years,

2445
01:34:29.748 --> 01:34:32.402
I said mom I'm not eating meat anymore.

2446
01:34:32.402 --> 01:34:36.488
And I was vegan right into the military.

2447
01:34:36.488 --> 01:34:39.707
I was mostly I got call a fruitarian

2448
01:34:39.707 --> 01:34:41.751
and I remember in the military they said,

2449
01:34:41.751 --> 01:34:44.015
oh, you're gonna get osteoporosis and you're gonna,

2450
01:34:44.015 --> 01:34:45.522
you're not gonna do well in this side.

2451
01:34:45.522 --> 01:34:48.645
I mean I was the top of my game in the military.

2452
01:34:48.645 --> 01:34:50.517
You're like, I'm the one training you.

2453
01:34:50.517 --> 01:34:51.908
Yeah.
How to do this.

2454
01:34:51.908 --> 01:34:55.622
But it was more the doctor saying that but I think

2455
01:34:55.622 --> 01:34:58.488
as I think I told you earlier is that a lot of the soldiers

2456
01:34:58.488 --> 01:35:02.269
like the commando soldiers were vegetarians.

2457
01:35:02.269 --> 01:35:04.129
Like the military really catered,

2458
01:35:04.129 --> 01:35:06.144
you didn't have that in Canada.

2459
01:35:06.144 --> 01:35:07.752
I think there was a whole section in the military

2460
01:35:07.752 --> 01:35:09.289
and the food room called the food room

2461
01:35:09.289 --> 01:35:11.751
where the food is made for the soldiers.

2462
01:35:11.751 --> 01:35:15.008
Half the food room is all vegan options you know.

2463
01:35:15.008 --> 01:35:18.210
And I was amazed during that time

2464
01:35:18.210 --> 01:35:20.085
that a lot of soldiers higher...

2465
01:35:20.085 --> 01:35:23.575
Even a lot of the athletes, they were not necessarily

2466
01:35:23.575 --> 01:35:25.047
just plant-based but just vegetarians

2467
01:35:25.047 --> 01:35:28.842
or didn't eat eggs, dairy or whatnot.

2468
01:35:28.842 --> 01:35:32.818
And then I moved back to Canada and on the national team,

2469
01:35:32.818 --> 01:35:37.818
the issue was, is I didn't have the food that I needed.

2470
01:35:38.024 --> 01:35:40.826
Like when we're in Europe, they fed you.

2471
01:35:40.826 --> 01:35:43.556
So what they would give me was white bread,

2472
01:35:43.556 --> 01:35:44.754
cheese and whatnot.

2473
01:35:44.754 --> 01:35:46.571
So the dietician there said,

2474
01:35:46.571 --> 01:35:48.406
you have to start eating a little bit of meat

2475
01:35:48.406 --> 01:35:50.670
and whatnot to get your strength back.

2476
01:35:50.670 --> 01:35:54.402
And so I did, I flipped back and I started eating meat,

2477
01:35:54.402 --> 01:35:56.597
not feeling too great but not feeling too great either

2478
01:35:56.597 --> 01:35:59.841
because you can be vegetarian and not healthy

2479
01:35:59.841 --> 01:36:02.008
by eating the wrong foods.

2480
01:36:03.435 --> 01:36:05.360
And then I remember watching the Game Changer

2481
01:36:05.360 --> 01:36:08.499
and I saw Dotsie there and I go, what the hell?

2482
01:36:08.499 --> 01:36:10.771
And so I remember, and I called her and I said Dotsie,

2483
01:36:10.771 --> 01:36:12.133
thank God I saw you, you know what I mean?

2484
01:36:12.133 --> 01:36:15.201
And I just, my dad, you're just watched this documentary.

2485
01:36:15.201 --> 01:36:17.555
And I thought what an idiot I am, you know what I mean?

2486
01:36:17.555 --> 01:36:20.811
And as soon as I did, everything went up,

2487
01:36:20.811 --> 01:36:24.420
my recovery was better, I rode stronger, I rode faster.

2488
01:36:24.420 --> 01:36:26.492
I felt great, I stepped better, you know what I mean?

2489
01:36:26.492 --> 01:36:27.855
So, yeah.

2490
01:36:27.855 --> 01:36:29.554
So it was kinda like in waves.

2491
01:36:29.554 --> 01:36:30.387
Sure.

2492
01:36:32.365 --> 01:36:34.849
But Game Changers only came out a couple of years ago.

2493
01:36:34.849 --> 01:36:37.721
So you only deployed that plant-based diet

2494
01:36:37.721 --> 01:36:39.830
in the most recent ramp.

2495
01:36:39.830 --> 01:36:41.569
About two and a half years ago, yeah.

2496
01:36:41.569 --> 01:36:43.067
This most recent one, correct, yes.

2497
01:36:43.067 --> 01:36:44.067
Yeah, yeah.

2498
01:36:44.067 --> 01:36:45.419
And you could feel a difference.

2499
01:36:45.419 --> 01:36:46.252
Oh, for sure.

2500
01:36:46.252 --> 01:36:48.283
I mean I know how I felt back in.

2501
01:36:48.283 --> 01:36:51.971
It depends what your options are to eat.

2502
01:36:51.971 --> 01:36:53.853
And just going that route.

2503
01:36:53.853 --> 01:36:55.646
I mean instantly within the week,

2504
01:36:55.646 --> 01:36:58.409
I said, this is how I felt when I was in the military

2505
01:36:58.409 --> 01:37:00.509
and you forget about that.

2506
01:37:00.509 --> 01:37:03.581
But mind you even when I was eating, I didn't eat beef

2507
01:37:03.581 --> 01:37:05.139
or anything, just a little bit of chicken and fish.

2508
01:37:05.139 --> 01:37:09.097
I still didn't feel good or didn't feel...

2509
01:37:09.097 --> 01:37:11.673
'Cause I do it for humanitarian reasons.

2510
01:37:11.673 --> 01:37:13.475
At that time I wasn't thinking about the health-

2511
01:37:13.475 --> 01:37:15.900
Right, you'd be doing it irrespective of

2512
01:37:15.900 --> 01:37:19.711
how it impacted your athletic performance.

2513
01:37:19.711 --> 01:37:21.269
But yeah, I mean, I'm a big believer,

2514
01:37:21.269 --> 01:37:22.671
I don't push it on people or whatever.

2515
01:37:22.671 --> 01:37:24.106
Yeah, you're low key about it.

2516
01:37:24.106 --> 01:37:26.438
Like you're not out on the pulpit.

2517
01:37:26.438 --> 01:37:27.771
No, no.

2518
01:37:27.771 --> 01:37:29.645
Banging the drum about this.
No.

2519
01:37:29.645 --> 01:37:30.478
I mean to each their own,

2520
01:37:30.478 --> 01:37:32.096
everybody knows what's good and what's right.

2521
01:37:32.096 --> 01:37:34.124
I'm not there to tell you what to do

2522
01:37:34.124 --> 01:37:36.452
but I know what works for me and what makes me feel good.

2523
01:37:36.452 --> 01:37:37.659
You know so.

2524
01:37:37.659 --> 01:37:39.222
Have you met Dotsie?

2525
01:37:39.222 --> 01:37:40.384
Oh yeah, we raced together.

2526
01:37:40.384 --> 01:37:41.217
Oh you did.
Yeah, yeah.

2527
01:37:41.217 --> 01:37:42.647
So you know her from the racing years?

2528
01:37:42.647 --> 01:37:44.558
Yeah, yeah, I know her.
Oh that's cool.

2529
01:37:44.558 --> 01:37:45.391
Yeah.

2530
01:37:45.391 --> 01:37:46.588
She's the best.
Yeah, yeah.

2531
01:37:46.588 --> 01:37:48.101
I totally know her year.

2532
01:37:48.101 --> 01:37:49.401
It's awesome, yeah.
That's cool.

2533
01:37:49.401 --> 01:37:51.253
It's like she slapped me through the TV right?

2534
01:37:51.253 --> 01:37:52.216
Yeah.

2535
01:37:52.216 --> 01:37:53.347
So it was meant to be.

2536
01:37:53.347 --> 01:37:54.352
But I know her.

2537
01:37:54.352 --> 01:37:55.932
Yeah.

2538
01:37:55.932 --> 01:37:57.192
Wow.

2539
01:37:57.192 --> 01:38:01.314
So what is a day in the life of food look like for you

2540
01:38:01.314 --> 01:38:03.071
when you're training really hard.

2541
01:38:03.071 --> 01:38:03.965
When I'm training really hard?

2542
01:38:03.965 --> 01:38:05.841
Well I do a lot of smoothies,

2543
01:38:05.841 --> 01:38:08.465
I like to stick to liquids on the bike as much as possible.

2544
01:38:08.465 --> 01:38:11.972
You're pretty much liquid during RAAM throughout,

2545
01:38:11.972 --> 01:38:13.315
you just can't...

2546
01:38:13.315 --> 01:38:15.722
Especially in the heat to be able to keep down-

2547
01:38:15.722 --> 01:38:17.269
I couldn't eat anything, no.

2548
01:38:17.269 --> 01:38:18.218
And if I could, it's just fruit,

2549
01:38:18.218 --> 01:38:20.308
like watermelon is big, grapes are big

2550
01:38:20.308 --> 01:38:22.006
like the juicy fruits kind of stuff you know.

2551
01:38:22.006 --> 01:38:23.314
What about dates?

2552
01:38:23.314 --> 01:38:24.851
Dates, I didn't use dates.

2553
01:38:24.851 --> 01:38:27.951
I mean, I use it in training but not so much in the race.

2554
01:38:27.951 --> 01:38:31.261
Hammer is my, they have a lot of hammer, vegan bars

2555
01:38:31.261 --> 01:38:35.472
and stuff, they have awesome vegan protein powders

2556
01:38:35.472 --> 01:38:37.150
or whatever.
I like how you call vegan.

2557
01:38:37.150 --> 01:38:40.537
Or vegetarian, vegan, vegan, it's a Canadian thing.

2558
01:38:40.537 --> 01:38:42.992
(both laughs)

2559
01:38:42.992 --> 01:38:43.982
So yeah I mean-

2560
01:38:43.982 --> 01:38:45.846
But in training like walk me through

2561
01:38:45.846 --> 01:38:48.745
like the real foods that you're eating.

2562
01:38:48.745 --> 01:38:51.750
Well, I mean they would give me wraps,

2563
01:38:51.750 --> 01:38:56.417
tofu scramble wraps, avocado was a big one, rice, beans.

2564
01:38:58.789 --> 01:39:00.260
But keeping it simple.

2565
01:39:00.260 --> 01:39:02.217
Just keep that, they just in little wraps

2566
01:39:02.217 --> 01:39:03.497
when giving me from the bike,

2567
01:39:03.497 --> 01:39:05.544
like you're doing everything on the bike.

2568
01:39:05.544 --> 01:39:08.106
So, just simple light foods.

2569
01:39:08.106 --> 01:39:09.602
'Cause again, you don't have a huge appetite,

2570
01:39:09.602 --> 01:39:10.435
you just don't.

2571
01:39:10.435 --> 01:39:13.253
If you just keep it consistent, a lot of fluids.

2572
01:39:13.253 --> 01:39:14.291
Again in that kind of heat,

2573
01:39:14.291 --> 01:39:16.675
as you know, it's really difficult to use.

2574
01:39:16.675 --> 01:39:18.610 line:15%
And the training, I mean you're on the bike

2575
01:39:18.610 --> 01:39:20.360 line:15%
like 60 hours a week.

2576
01:39:21.625 --> 01:39:24.768 line:15%
But you live where it gets cold.

2577
01:39:24.768 --> 01:39:26.806
How much of that is indoors on a trainer

2578
01:39:26.806 --> 01:39:28.107
versus out on the road.

2579
01:39:28.107 --> 01:39:31.839
In the winter, it's 100% indoors because I live in Vernon.

2580
01:39:31.839 --> 01:39:33.282
So you cannot ride out.

2581
01:39:33.282 --> 01:39:34.428
I mean, you can with the snow bike,

2582
01:39:34.428 --> 01:39:35.457
I know the big fat tires.

2583
01:39:35.457 --> 01:39:36.839
Yeah.
I don't do that.

2584
01:39:36.839 --> 01:39:38.431
So, but I'll start...

2585
01:39:38.431 --> 01:39:40.301
Like I'll cross train with running,

2586
01:39:40.301 --> 01:39:41.721
like you know trail running ice,

2587
01:39:41.721 --> 01:39:43.321
it's called crampons or little spikes

2588
01:39:43.321 --> 01:39:45.597
on the bottom of your shoes or whatever so, I'll do that

2589
01:39:45.597 --> 01:39:47.632
or I'll drive to Vancouver, my parents live there.

2590
01:39:47.632 --> 01:39:50.906
You can ride there it's very rainy but you can ride outside.

2591
01:39:50.906 --> 01:39:53.000
But I'm gonna say trainer workouts in the winter,

2592
01:39:53.000 --> 01:39:55.981
it's probably about 70 to 80% for me.

2593
01:39:55.981 --> 01:39:59.188
And how do you deal with just the mental doldrums of that?

2594
01:39:59.188 --> 01:40:00.021
That's RAAM.

2595
01:40:00.021 --> 01:40:02.138
Like do you watch movies and listen to music or-

2596
01:40:02.138 --> 01:40:03.156
Oh sorry no way I don't.

2597
01:40:03.156 --> 01:40:05.442
Just a complete deprivation tank?

2598
01:40:05.442 --> 01:40:07.089
Don't listen to music, don't look at my phone.

2599
01:40:07.089 --> 01:40:08.840
Not your suffering.

2600
01:40:08.840 --> 01:40:10.879
Yeah, no television, no nothing

2601
01:40:10.879 --> 01:40:12.577
so I don't get that when I race.

2602
01:40:12.577 --> 01:40:16.102
Like in Kansas all you see is road for 20 hours

2603
01:40:16.102 --> 01:40:17.197
so I guess-

2604
01:40:17.197 --> 01:40:19.382
That's another level of discipline

2605
01:40:19.382 --> 01:40:21.891
'cause it would be so easy to just set up a laptop

2606
01:40:21.891 --> 01:40:24.103
in front of you and watch something while you're doing it.

2607
01:40:24.103 --> 01:40:26.030
No, like I remember like even sometimes,

2608
01:40:26.030 --> 01:40:27.578
downstairs if somebody turns on the TV,

2609
01:40:27.578 --> 01:40:30.124
I almost find it like a distraction.

2610
01:40:30.124 --> 01:40:31.298
I just try to-

2611
01:40:31.298 --> 01:40:32.558
So where does your mind go?

2612
01:40:32.558 --> 01:40:34.013
It just goes to what I'm doing

2613
01:40:34.013 --> 01:40:36.527
like I'll replicate a course or a ride or whatever,

2614
01:40:36.527 --> 01:40:39.093
like I'll do like a replicated climb.

2615
01:40:39.093 --> 01:40:41.860
On K, I'm gonna ride flat here for 20 K or whatever,

2616
01:40:41.860 --> 01:40:44.518
they'll climb for two hours and I'll go down

2617
01:40:44.518 --> 01:40:45.351
to the flat skin.

2618
01:40:45.351 --> 01:40:47.433
So I'll play things in my head

2619
01:40:47.433 --> 01:40:49.550
but that's Race Across America,

2620
01:40:49.550 --> 01:40:51.126
you just have to be in robot mode

2621
01:40:51.126 --> 01:40:52.878
and just keep pedaling looking forward

2622
01:40:52.878 --> 01:40:54.621
and don't think about anything else.

2623
01:40:54.621 --> 01:40:57.579
Time becomes very fluid and malleable

2624
01:40:57.579 --> 01:40:59.845
like when you've been doing that for a while

2625
01:40:59.845 --> 01:41:01.728
and you increase your volume,

2626
01:41:01.728 --> 01:41:06.098
suddenly what used to feel like forever feels like nothing.

2627
01:41:06.098 --> 01:41:08.459
Like an eight hour ride can feel like a two-hour ride

2628
01:41:08.459 --> 01:41:10.361
when you acclimated yourself to it.

2629
01:41:10.361 --> 01:41:13.045
But it does require that level of discipline.

2630
01:41:13.045 --> 01:41:14.319
Well, I mean it's training too.

2631
01:41:14.319 --> 01:41:15.723
Like I remember it's funny that you say that,

2632
01:41:15.723 --> 01:41:18.256
'cause sometimes like veering the end of my training,

2633
01:41:18.256 --> 01:41:20.523
I'd be so I go, yay, I only have to do 10 hours today.

2634
01:41:20.523 --> 01:41:22.770
Yeah, it's all perspective. (Rich laughs)

2635
01:41:22.770 --> 01:41:25.016
But that's better than 20 hours.

2636
01:41:25.016 --> 01:41:26.723
So that's funny that you say that

2637
01:41:26.723 --> 01:41:29.467
and like going for a four hour ride is a warmup,

2638
01:41:29.467 --> 01:41:30.466
you know what I mean?

2639
01:41:30.466 --> 01:41:32.350
So, again like you said, it's in perspective

2640
01:41:32.350 --> 01:41:33.726
but for around, that's what you're gonna do

2641
01:41:33.726 --> 01:41:36.265
for 9, 10, 11, 12 days.

2642
01:41:36.265 --> 01:41:38.276
So, it's better that you training

2643
01:41:38.276 --> 01:41:39.709
and you know what that feels like

2644
01:41:39.709 --> 01:41:42.617
rather than experiencing that in the race.

2645
01:41:42.617 --> 01:41:46.367
Talk to me about how martial arts interplay

2646
01:41:47.969 --> 01:41:51.066
with endurance sports like I'm interested in

2647
01:41:51.066 --> 01:41:54.304
what you've kind of gleaned from being a martial artist,

2648
01:41:54.304 --> 01:41:59.304
a combat, athlete and you know what lessons from that world

2649
01:41:59.790 --> 01:42:03.521
are applicable to ultra endurance and vice versa.

2650
01:42:03.521 --> 01:42:06.757
Like how do those worlds compliment each other?

2651
01:42:06.757 --> 01:42:09.265
Well I think it's discipline and consistency.

2652
01:42:09.265 --> 01:42:12.100
Like with combat sports and Taekwondo or whatever

2653
01:42:12.100 --> 01:42:14.674
or martial arts, you have to train and practice

2654
01:42:14.674 --> 01:42:16.655
'cause it's a skilled sport.

2655
01:42:16.655 --> 01:42:19.086
Writing is more, you're just peddling, you know what I mean?

2656
01:42:19.086 --> 01:42:20.840
It's a mental thing of but you have to practice

2657
01:42:20.840 --> 01:42:21.776
and again pecans.

2658
01:42:21.776 --> 01:42:23.755
And even when you're having those days

2659
01:42:23.755 --> 01:42:25.923
where you just don't wanna do it, you have to do it.

2660
01:42:25.923 --> 01:42:28.577
'Cause you're not always gonna have those great days.

2661
01:42:28.577 --> 01:42:30.686
Few and far beyond, you're gonna have those great days.

2662
01:42:30.686 --> 01:42:33.718
So I think it's a matter of again of the consistency

2663
01:42:33.718 --> 01:42:37.661
and no matter where you are, how you feel

2664
01:42:37.661 --> 01:42:40.886
that you gotta do whatever you have to do.

2665
01:42:40.886 --> 01:42:42.406
A key difference being,

2666
01:42:42.406 --> 01:42:46.055
when you're a combat sports athlete, you have an opponent.

2667
01:42:46.055 --> 01:42:48.984
But in endurance, really the opponent is you.

2668
01:42:48.984 --> 01:42:50.114
Like, you're not..

2669
01:42:50.114 --> 01:42:51.431
There are other people in the race,

2670
01:42:51.431 --> 01:42:55.250
but it's kind of irrelevant because it's a contest

2671
01:42:55.250 --> 01:42:56.767
between you and yourself.

2672
01:42:56.767 --> 01:42:57.985
Exactly.

2673
01:42:57.985 --> 01:43:00.287
There's nothing more difficult than kickboxing,

2674
01:43:00.287 --> 01:43:01.120
I'll tell you that much.

2675
01:43:01.120 --> 01:43:04.114
Exactly what you said because you have more control

2676
01:43:04.114 --> 01:43:06.535
when you're doing cycling or whatever, you can stop it

2677
01:43:06.535 --> 01:43:08.987
but if you stop when you're fighting someone,

2678
01:43:08.987 --> 01:43:10.459
they'll beat you up, you know what I mean?

2679
01:43:10.459 --> 01:43:13.170
That you have to be one op on that.

2680
01:43:13.170 --> 01:43:16.782
So, I found it way more challenging and way more important

2681
01:43:16.782 --> 01:43:19.694
to be on top of your game in that particular sport,

2682
01:43:19.694 --> 01:43:22.118
because the objective of that sport is

2683
01:43:22.118 --> 01:43:23.581
to knock the other person down.

2684
01:43:23.581 --> 01:43:24.414
Sure.

2685
01:43:24.414 --> 01:43:28.136
And also if you drop your guard, you can get injured-

2686
01:43:28.136 --> 01:43:29.226
In one second.

2687
01:43:29.226 --> 01:43:30.996
Like you can just get off your bike,

2688
01:43:30.996 --> 01:43:33.777
I mean, maybe you fall asleep on your bike and you tip over.

2689
01:43:33.777 --> 01:43:37.666
That's not so good or you crash descending 80 miles an hour

2690
01:43:37.666 --> 01:43:41.840
and peel your face off but generally the stakes

2691
01:43:41.840 --> 01:43:44.477
are a little bit more dire and higher

2692
01:43:44.477 --> 01:43:45.813
in a combat mission of course.

2693
01:43:45.813 --> 01:43:48.559
Oh for sure, there is no comparison.

2694
01:43:48.559 --> 01:43:49.392
Yeah.

2695
01:43:49.392 --> 01:43:54.392
So they're very different in that regard but the discipline,

2696
01:43:56.077 --> 01:43:59.244
the attention to detail and precision.

2697
01:44:00.992 --> 01:44:04.032
In ultra endurance, you can kinda...

2698
01:44:04.032 --> 01:44:07.248
Because it's so long, you're not gonna be perfect.

2699
01:44:07.248 --> 01:44:12.066
So precision is less applicable but you have to stay

2700
01:44:12.066 --> 01:44:14.394
on top of all those details like saddle sores

2701
01:44:14.394 --> 01:44:16.367
and your nutrition schedule and all of that

2702
01:44:16.367 --> 01:44:20.155
like there's a rigor to that I think that is demanding

2703
01:44:20.155 --> 01:44:24.196
and essential if you're gonna compete to your potential.

2704
01:44:24.196 --> 01:44:25.906
But it becomes almost systematic

2705
01:44:25.906 --> 01:44:28.014
like you know the routine, you know what...

2706
01:44:28.014 --> 01:44:29.327
Like you said, there's way more elements

2707
01:44:29.327 --> 01:44:31.287
in ultra endurance racing.

2708
01:44:31.287 --> 01:44:32.863
It's not just a matter of doing one thing,

2709
01:44:32.863 --> 01:44:34.889
like you said, it's nutrition, it's clothing,

2710
01:44:34.889 --> 01:44:36.922
it's your bike, it's everything.

2711
01:44:36.922 --> 01:44:38.431
But it becomes almost like second nature.

2712
01:44:38.431 --> 01:44:42.047
That's why being consistent with it

2713
01:44:42.047 --> 01:44:46.307
and not doing the training even when you don't feel like it

2714
01:44:46.307 --> 01:44:49.418
is important and staying on top of your game.

2715
01:44:49.418 --> 01:44:53.228
And always pushing harder than you wanna push.

2716
01:44:53.228 --> 01:44:57.196
Like I try to train harder than I would in a race.

2717
01:44:57.196 --> 01:44:58.924
So the race almost feels easy.

2718
01:44:58.924 --> 01:45:00.316
Not just a matter of sitting on your bike

2719
01:45:00.316 --> 01:45:01.868
and peddling away mindlessly,

2720
01:45:01.868 --> 01:45:04.217
there's a purpose to what you're doing.

2721
01:45:04.217 --> 01:45:05.909
And I think for me that's really important,

2722
01:45:05.909 --> 01:45:07.828
it means to prepare yourself especially

2723
01:45:07.828 --> 01:45:09.012
when you're going after a goal,

2724
01:45:09.012 --> 01:45:10.778
like we're going after a 10 day goal.

2725
01:45:10.778 --> 01:45:12.159
So I have to be come to that line

2726
01:45:12.159 --> 01:45:14.223
in the best shape possible.

2727
01:45:14.223 --> 01:45:16.879
And that requires a lot of training, specific training,

2728
01:45:16.879 --> 01:45:19.116
being on top of it and consistency.

2729
01:45:19.116 --> 01:45:21.141
That posts who, okay, I just wanna finish in 12 days,

2730
01:45:21.141 --> 01:45:22.811
that's a whole different ball game.

2731
01:45:22.811 --> 01:45:25.957
But what I'm going after is a little bit more intense.

2732
01:45:25.957 --> 01:45:27.472
So the precision, organization

2733
01:45:27.472 --> 01:45:29.699
and having a good team behind you like my crew has

2734
01:45:29.699 --> 01:45:31.713
to be on the same page as me.

2735
01:45:31.713 --> 01:45:33.895
People don't realize how important.

2736
01:45:33.895 --> 01:45:36.630
Your finish is determined by your crew,

2737
01:45:36.630 --> 01:45:37.463
how good your crew is.

2738
01:45:37.463 --> 01:45:38.693
Right, it is a team sport in that sense.

2739
01:45:38.693 --> 01:45:41.220
It's a total team sport.

2740
01:45:41.220 --> 01:45:43.884
And you think about putting nine people together, right.

2741
01:45:43.884 --> 01:45:45.329
Oh there's legendary stories-

2742
01:45:45.329 --> 01:45:46.329
Oh my God.

2743
01:45:46.329 --> 01:45:47.849
Of crews blowing up and leaving-

2744
01:45:47.849 --> 01:45:49.136
Oh yeah, we had one.

2745
01:45:49.136 --> 01:45:50.826
I mean, you'll see,

2746
01:45:50.826 --> 01:45:52.064
I mean, we have a documentary coming out,

2747
01:45:52.064 --> 01:45:54.879
you'll see one crew member taking off in the car.

2748
01:45:54.879 --> 01:45:56.705
He just took off.

2749
01:45:56.705 --> 01:46:00.070
So, I mean it's crazy, the dynamics that go in,

2750
01:46:00.070 --> 01:46:02.997
like that's a whole new podcast where you wreck it all.

2751
01:46:02.997 --> 01:46:06.221
You would think like we're in an RV or we're in a van,

2752
01:46:06.221 --> 01:46:07.976
we're going all the way across the country,

2753
01:46:07.976 --> 01:46:08.859
this person's on a bike.

2754
01:46:08.859 --> 01:46:09.693
Like, what are we gonna do?

2755
01:46:09.693 --> 01:46:11.485
We're gonna crank the tunes, we're gonna kick back,

2756
01:46:11.485 --> 01:46:12.344
it's gonna be awesome.

2757
01:46:12.344 --> 01:46:15.043
But literally like you're taking bottle drops

2758
01:46:15.043 --> 01:46:17.890
like every 15 minutes or whatever

2759
01:46:17.890 --> 01:46:20.142
and they have to sleep too.

2760
01:46:20.142 --> 01:46:23.236
Were you rotating crew members between multiple vehicles.

2761
01:46:23.236 --> 01:46:27.500
No, we had two sets of crew members, three and three.

2762
01:46:27.500 --> 01:46:28.862
So they do 12 hour shifts,

2763
01:46:28.862 --> 01:46:30.776
you had a day shift and then a night shift. Right.

2764
01:46:30.776 --> 01:46:32.844
So at least it gives them a bit of time too

2765
01:46:32.844 --> 01:46:35.078
but still there's lots fill up the gas,

2766
01:46:35.078 --> 01:46:39.083
think of the navigation they have to eat too.

2767
01:46:39.083 --> 01:46:41.306
To keep me prepared, know about the terrain,

2768
01:46:41.306 --> 01:46:43.245
know about who's, where, or whatever,

2769
01:46:43.245 --> 01:46:44.618
there's a lot of things going on,

2770
01:46:44.618 --> 01:46:48.392
you can ask any crew member that those 12 hours just fly by.

2771
01:46:48.392 --> 01:46:50.516
